{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DaLGDTJRCa67nvbsRGKJQj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — nlp_variance_gtheory\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28 — 9 weeks from today)\n5\t- Backup: ACL 2027 via ARR (October 15 cycle — 21 weeks)\n6\t- Extended: TACL (rolling)\n7\t\n8\t## Title\n9\tReproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n12\t\n13\t**Claim 1 — Item Dominance (benchmark-conditional)**: Item selection is the single largest variance source in multiple-choice LLM evaluation (MVP: 58.4% on MMLU single-model; expected to decrease with model-as-facet reanalysis). Its magnitude is hypothesized to be benchmark-dependent — weakening on reasoning tasks where temperature/seed interactions increase — but this gradient requires exp-002 confirmation. Item dominance is expected from measurement theory when item difficulty varies widely (Brennan, 2001); our contribution is in (a) quantifying the precise magnitude across benchmarks and models, (b) demonstrating whether a systematic benchmark-dependent gradient exists, and (c) deriving D-study implications specific to LLM evaluation.\n14\t**Fallback narratives**: (A) If gradient confirmed → \"benchmark-conditional variance structure\"; (B) If gradient not significant → \"item dominance is universal but magnitude varies\"; (C) If model-as-facet reduces item_id below 30% → pivot to \"model×item interaction is the dominant source\" — narrative direction changes substantially.\n15\t\n16\t**Claim 2 — Conditional Separability**: Infrastructure factors (numerical precision) and methodology factors (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) are approximately separable at the aggregate level — direct infrastructure×methodology interaction ≈0% under binary scoring; the only detectable cross-factor interaction (precision×temperature=1.3%) emerges under text-exact-match scoring, consistent with floating-point non-associativity (Messina & Scotta, 2026). Both factor types interact substantially with item difficulty (precision×item, prompt×item), making their effects item-conditional. This provides a metric-qualified confirmatory finding for isolated-factor studies while warning that conclusions are conditional on item selection and evaluation metric. Note: with only numerical precision tested on a single GPU architecture (pending exp-003 for hardware crossing), \"infrastructure\" is currently limited to the precision facet.\n17\t\n18\t**Claim 3 — Metric-Conditional Reproducibility Budget**: Reproducibility budgets are fundamentally metric-dependent: binary correct scoring yields G=0.357 (item dominance makes G≥0.90 unreachable without massive item expansion), while text-exact-match scoring yields G=0.975 (already adequate). D-study projections show that increasing item sampling is more cost-effective than increasing prompt/seed replication, but the optimal allocation depends critically on both the evaluation metric and the benchmark. Current common practices (1 prompt, 1 seed, ≤200 items, single metric) produce misleading reliability estimates.\n19\t\n20\t## Contributions (3 条，明确区分)\n21\t\n22\t1. **Cross-benchmark variance gradient**: The first multi-benchmark G-theory analysis showing that variance structure systematically shifts from item-dominated (knowledge retrieval) to interaction-dominated (reasoning tasks), explaining why prior single-benchmark studies reached seemingly contradictory conclusions.\n23\t\n24\t2. **Confirmatory separability test**: Infrastructure×methodology interactions are negligible at the aggregate level (<0.5% under binary scoring), providing empirical support for isolated-factor studies — with the caveat that both factors interact with items and the finding is metric-conditional.\n25\t\n26\t3. **Task-specific reproducibility budgets**: D-study projections with bootstrap CIs yield actionable, benchmark-specific item/prompt/seed allocations for achieving target reliability (G≥0.90), contextualized against IRT-based subsampling approaches (tinyBenchmarks, HELM-Lite).\n27\t\n28\t## Story Spine\n29\t\n30\t**Problem**: When LLM evaluation results vary across reproductions, practitioners cannot determine whether the culprit is prompt phrasing, random seed, GPU hardware, numerical precision, temperature, or item selection — let alone how these factors interact. Every prior study isolates at most two sources, implicitly assuming separability.\n31\t\n32\t**Existing approaches & gaps**: Bouthillier et al. (2021) decomposed ML benchmark variance across hyperparameters, data splits, and seeds via ANOVA, establishing the template for factorial variance decomposition in ML evaluation. Mesting (2026) adapted G-theory to 5 methodological facets in LLM evaluation but treated infrastructure as fixed. Yuan et al. (2025) documented up to 9% accuracy variation from hardware/precision without factorial attribution. Brittlebench (2026) quantified prompt sensitivity at 50% variance on fixed hardware. IRT-based approaches (Lalor et al. 2019; Rodriguez et al. 2021; Polo et al. 2024) have analyzed item-level properties and efficient subsampling, but from a different methodological perspective than G-theory. No study has: (a) crossed infrastructure and methodology in a unified G-theory framework, (b) compared variance structure across multiple benchmarks, or (c) provided metric-conditional reproducibility budgets.\n33\t\n34\t**Our framing**: We conduct a multi-benchmark G-theory study crossing infrastructure (numerical precision) × methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) × measurement (item) factors, with model treated as a crossed facet. The infrastructure crossing yields a confirmatory null result: aggregate separability holds, validating the implicit assumption of prior isolated-factor studies. The primary novel finding is the cross-benchmark variance gradient — item dominance systematically weakens as tasks shift from knowledge retrieval to multi-step reasoning — explaining divergent conclusions in the literature. D-study projections provide the first benchmark-specific, metric-conditional reproducibility budgets for LLM evaluation, directly comparable to IRT-based subsampling approaches.\n35\t\n36\t**Headline result template**: \"In a G-study of K conditions across M models and N benchmarks, item selection is the largest single variance source on multiple-choice benchmarks (X-Y%, benchmark-dependent [placeholder: fill after exp-001/002]), while direct infrastructure×methodology interactions are negligible. Variance structure [is/is not] benchmark-conditional [pending exp-002]. D-study reproducibility budgets show that optimal facet allocation is both benchmark-specific and metric-conditional.\"\n37\t\n38\t## Hook (Counter-Intuitive Quantified Claim)\n39\t\"Researchers debating whether to standardize prompts, seeds, or GPU hardware for LLM evaluation implicitly assume that their findings will generalize across benchmarks — our G-theory analysis across N benchmarks shows the variance structure itself is benchmark-conditional, rendering any 'universal reproducibility recipe' illusory.\"\n40\t\n41\t## Narrative Strategy\n42\t- **Type**: Cross-domain transfer (psychometrics G-theory → ML evaluation) + Empirical contribution (benchmark-conditional gradient)\n43\t- **Tension**: \"No universal recipe\" — the field seeks standardization but variance structure varies qualitatively across task types\n44\t- **Intro structure**: 4 paragraphs + 3 contributions (field mode from distilled writing patterns)\n45\t- **Signature visual**: Stacked-bar variance decomposition chart across benchmarks (field idiom from Mesting/Brittlebench)\n46\t- **Figure plan** (4 figures minimum):\n47\t  1. Problem illustration: same model, different benchmark → different dominant variance source\n48\t  2. G-theory facet diagram showing crossed design\n49\t  3. Stacked-bar variance decomposition across benchmarks (signature figure)\n50\t  4. D-study projection curves: G vs item count for different benchmarks\n51\t- **Key framing shifts from Dream feedback**:\n52\t  - Infrastructure crossing framed as **confirmatory** (validates isolated-factor studies), not as primary novelty\n53\t  - Item dominance acknowledged as **psychometrically expected**; contribution is quantification + benchmark gradient\n54\t  - Cross-benchmark gradient is the **primary novel finding**\n55\t  - \"Fully crossed\" language qualified to MMLU exp-001 only; other benchmarks use reduced designs\n56\t\n57\t## Venue Fit Risks\n58\t- AAAI: Strong fit for \"evaluation methodology\" track. Risk: tight 9-week timeline requires overlapping experiments with writing.\n59\t- Reviewer concern 1: \"Only open-weight models, not API models\" — We intentionally study open-weight models where infrastructure is controllable; API models (studied by Mesting) have opaque infrastructure. Frame as complementary.\n60\t- Reviewer concern 2: \"G-theory is borrowed methodology, not novel\" — Frame as cross-domain transfer. The novelty is the cross-benchmark variance gradient finding, not G-theory itself.\n61\t- Reviewer concern 3: \"Item dominance is obvious from measurement theory\" — Acknowledge explicitly. Contribution is quantification (X-Y%, pending exp-001/002), benchmark-dependence, and D-study implications. Contextualize against IRT item analysis literature.\n62\t\n63\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Planned Responses\n64\t1. **\"Only N GPU types\"** → exp-003 (multi-hardware crossing) addresses this. If blocked, explicitly scope to \"numerical precision\" and frame hardware as future work.\n65\t2. **\"float32 engine confound\"** → exp-001 fixes with V0 enforce_eager for all precisions. Confound discovery itself is a standalone methodological contribution.\n66\t3. **\"Only MMLU\"** → exp-002 expands to GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Cross-benchmark gradient is the core finding.\n67\t4. **\"Small item sample\"** → exp-001 expands from 50 to 200 items. exp-001b tests resampling stability.\n68\t5. **\"No training-time analysis\"** → exp-006 (fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa). Explicitly qualify as encoder-only classification; decoder fine-tuning is future work.\n69\t6. **\"Missing scale validation\"** → exp-005 (14B models)\n70\t7. **\"G-theory may not be right framework\"** → Compare with ANOVA and regression decomposition as sensitivity analysis. Cite Bouthillier et al. (2021) as ANOVA-based predecessor; show G-theory adds D-study projections.\n71\t8. **\"Separability claim is metric-dependent\"** → This is precisely the point: reproducibility budgets must be metric-qualified. Replace arbitrary threshold with bootstrap CI on interaction term.\n72\t9. **\"FP32 degradation is already known (Yuan 2025)\"** → Position as supporting finding with novel item-level breakdown. Not an independent claim.\n73\t10. **\"Incremental over Mesting (2026)\"** → Infrastructure crossing is confirmatory (valuable as validation, not breakthrough). Primary novelty: cross-benchmark gradient, task-specific budgets, dual-metric analysis, confound fix. Mesting = single benchmark, no infrastructure; we = multi-benchmark, infrastructure confirmation + benchmark gradient.\n74\t11. **\"Model not treated as G-theory facet\"** → exp-004 includes model-as-facet reanalysis of MMLU data. Report model×item interaction separately from residual.\n75\t12. **\"No CIs on variance components\"** → exp-004 includes bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) on all variance components in all tables.\n76\t13. **\"Missing related work — IRT, tinyBenchmarks, Bouthillier\"** → Comprehensive bibliography (25+ refs). Position D-study budgets against IRT-based subsampling. Cite Bouthillier et al. as ANOVA predecessor.\n77\t14. **\"Diagnostic tool claim unsubstantiated\"** → Release analysis pipeline code (G-theory variance decomposition + D-study projections) as reusable tool. Or reframe as \"empirical analysis + data release.\"\n78\t15. **\"Fine-tuning uses encoder-only RoBERTa\"** → Explicitly qualify. Decoder fine-tuning is a natural extension requiring substantially more compute.\n79\t\n80\t## Design Decisions\n81\t- **Judge model fixed**: Unlike Mesting who includes judge as a facet, we fix the judge to isolate infrastructure × methodology interactions. Judge variance (Mesting: 43.8%) would dominate and obscure the effects we study. Adding judge is a natural extension for future work.\n82\t- **Generation-based evaluation**: Following Hua et al. (EMNLP 2025), we avoid log-likelihood scoring to eliminate scoring artifacts that inflate apparent prompt sensitivity.\n83\t- **Default vLLM settings**: We use default vLLM settings (without batch-invariant kernels) to reflect realistic practitioner conditions, making CUDA-level nondeterminism a measurable variance source.\n84\t- **vLLM 0.9.2**: Required for Blackwell GPU (sm_100/sm_120) + CUDA 12.8 support.\n85\t- **Model as crossed facet**: In exp-001 analysis (exp-004), model is treated as a crossed random facet in G-theory, not pooled or stratified. This properly attributes model×item interaction and avoids inflating item_id estimates.\n86\t- **Reduced designs for non-MMLU benchmarks**: exp-002 uses 2 precisions × 2 temperatures (vs 3×3 in exp-001) due to GPU budget constraints. Explicitly described as \"reduced crossing\" rather than \"fully crossed.\"\n87\t- **Gemma2 float16 exclusion**: Gemma2 architecture does not support float16 inference (vLLM numerical instability guard). Cross-model analysis uses bf16+fp32 (balanced). fp16 data from Llama/Mistral/Qwen used in within-model analyses. bf16≈fp16 difference <0.1pp (D005).\n88\t\n89\t## Methodological Defenses (Dream #2 审稿问题逐条应对)\n90\t\n91\t### MD1. Binary Data in G-theory\n92\t**Issue**: Standard G-theory assumes continuous, approximately normal response variables. Binary 0/1 data violates this; item variance is mechanically linked to item difficulty via Bernoulli p(1-p).\n93\t**Defense strategy**: (a) Report correlation between Bernoulli variance p_i(1-p_i) and observed item variance — quantify how much item_id is \"mechanical\" vs informative; (b) Run parallel analysis on text_exact_match (continuous metric) to show variance structure is robust across metrics; (c) Discuss generalized G-theory (Brennan 2001 Ch.12) in Limitations; (d) Cite Lalor et al. (2019) who used IRT (also assumes latent continuous) on binary NLP data without this criticism being fatal.\n94\t\n95\t### MD2. Fixed vs Random Facet Sensitivity\n96\t**Issue**: Temperature (2-3 design points) and ordering (4 systematic rotations) are not randomly sampled from a universe — treating them as random facets may be inappropriate.\n97\t**Defense strategy**: Run sensitivity analysis with temperature and ordering as fixed facets. If variance decomposition changes <5%, report as robust. If substantial, discuss implications and present both analyses. This directly addresses Reviewer concern.\n98\t\n99\t### MD3. Separability Claim Scope\n100\t**Issue**: Separability (<0.3%) holds for knowledge benchmarks but not reasoning tasks (GSM8K=3.0%, MATH=2.5%).\n101\t**Defense**: Qualify all separability claims as \"under binary scoring on knowledge-retrieval benchmarks.\" Present benchmark-conditional separability as a finding (not a limitation): \"separability itself varies across task types.\"\n102\t\n103\t### MD4. D-study Optimization Documentation\n104\t**Issue**: Optimization procedure undocumented; Table 10 arithmetic anomaly (fewer items → higher G).\n105\t**Defense**: Document grid search procedure (bounds, step sizes, objective function). Verify and explain any non-monotonicity (likely due to joint optimization across facets where reallocating item budget to prompt/seed is more efficient).\n106\t\n107\t### MD5. G-coefficient Interpretation\n108\t**Issue**: G=0.22 for 200 items ≠ \"MMLU is unreliable\" (standard MMLU uses ~14K items).\n109\t**Defense**: (a) Clearly distinguish \"G for our 200-item subsample design\" from \"reliability of full MMLU\"; (b) Report D-study projection to 14K items; (c) Frame G=0.22 as \"cost of subsampling\" rather than \"MMLU is broken.\"\n110\t\n111\t### MD6. Bootstrap Independence\n112\t**Issue**: Item-level bootstrap with replacement creates ~127 unique items per 200-item resample, violating independence.\n113\t**Defense**: (a) Implement delete-1 jackknife as comparison; (b) If bootstrap and jackknife CIs are similar (within 20%), report as robust; (c) Discuss limitation in Methods.\n114\t\n115\t### MD7. IRT vs G-theory Comparison\n116\t**Issue**: tinyBenchmarks recommends ~100 items, D-study recommends 420. 4× discrepancy unexplained.\n117\t**Defense**: Direct comparison showing G-theory accounts for prompt/seed/ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. The 4× difference is the \"cost of multi-facet reliability\" — G-theory's budget is larger because it targets reliability across ALL facets, not just items. This comparison is a potential standalone contribution.\n118\t\n119\t### MD8. Cross-benchmark Design Asymmetry\n120\t**Issue**: MMLU uses 3 prec × 3 temp, other benchmarks use 2 × 2.\n121\t**Defense**: Re-analyze MMLU with matched 2×2 design (bf16/fp16 × T=0.0/T=0.7) and compare variance decomposition. If <5% change, report as robust to design reduction.\n122\t\n123\t### MD9. Title-Content Alignment\n124\t**Issue**: Title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but logprob analysis is minimal.\n125\t**Defense**: Expand logprob analysis across all benchmarks in exp-004, OR adjust title to \"...Across Benchmarks and Models.\"\n126\t\n127\t## Required Bibliography (minimum 25 references)\n128\tKey references to include beyond current literature_map:\n129\t- Bouthillier et al. (2021, JMLR) — ML variance decomposition via ANOVA\n130\t- Lalor et al. (2019, NAACL) — IRT-based item analysis in NLP\n131\t- Rodriguez et al. (2021, ACL) — IRT evaluation for NLP\n132\t- Polo et al. (2024) — tinyBenchmarks, IRT-based subsampling\n133\t- Perlitz et al. (2023) — Efficient LLM benchmarking\n134\t- Henderson et al. (2018) — Deep RL reproducibility, variance decomposition\n135\t- Shavelson & Webb (1991) — G-theory primer (canonical)\n136\t- Brennan (2001) — G-theory textbook (canonical)\n137\t- Lu et al. (2022) — Option ordering effects in MC evaluation\n138\t- Pezeshkpour & Hruschka (2024) — Option ordering effects\n139\t- Dodge et al. (2020, EMNLP) — Fine-tuning instability (seed, data order)\n140\t- Sellam et al. (2022) — MultiBERTs, multi-seed training\n141\t- Dehghani et al. (2021) — Benchmark Lottery\n142\t- Biderman et al. (2024) — Evaluation reproducibility lessons\n143\t- Vania et al. (2021) — IRT item properties in NLP\n144\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1ce775ab-4cde-4461-a6af-fd90e8f8da47","uuid":"4460f01d-d6ca-4836-9938-9c177b5db70f","timestamp":"2026-05-21T04:17:07.843Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T032813/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — nlp_variance_gtheory\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28 — 9 weeks from today)\n- Backup: ACL 2027 via ARR (October 15 cycle — 21 weeks)\n- Extended: TACL (rolling)\n\n## Title\nReproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\n\n## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n\n**Claim 1 — Item Dominance (benchmark-conditional)**: Item selection is the single largest variance source in multiple-choice LLM evaluation (MVP: 58.4% on MMLU single-model; expected to decrease with model-as-facet reanalysis). Its magnitude is hypothesized to be benchmark-dependent — weakening on reasoning tasks where temperature/seed interactions increase — but this gradient requires exp-002 confirmation. Item dominance is expected from measurement theory when item difficulty varies widely (Brennan, 2001); our contribution is in (a) quantifying the precise magnitude across benchmarks and models, (b) demonstrating whether a systematic benchmark-dependent gradient exists, and (c) deriving D-study implications specific to LLM evaluation.\n**Fallback narratives**: (A) If gradient confirmed → \"benchmark-conditional variance structure\"; (B) If gradient not significant → \"item dominance is universal but magnitude varies\"; (C) If model-as-facet reduces item_id below 30% → pivot to \"model×item interaction is the dominant source\" — narrative direction changes substantially.\n\n**Claim 2 — Conditional Separability**: Infrastructure factors (numerical precision) and methodology factors (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) are approximately separable at the aggregate level — direct infrastructure×methodology interaction ≈0% under binary scoring; the only detectable cross-factor interaction (precision×temperature=1.3%) emerges under text-exact-match scoring, consistent with floating-point non-associativity (Messina & Scotta, 2026). Both factor types interact substantially with item difficulty (precision×item, prompt×item), making their effects item-conditional. This provides a metric-qualified confirmatory finding for isolated-factor studies while warning that conclusions are conditional on item selection and evaluation metric. Note: with only numerical precision tested on a single GPU architecture (pending exp-003 for hardware crossing), \"infrastructure\" is currently limited to the precision facet.\n\n**Claim 3 — Metric-Conditional Reproducibility Budget**: Reproducibility budgets are fundamentally metric-dependent: binary correct scoring yields G=0.357 (item dominance makes G≥0.90 unreachable without massive item expansion), while text-exact-match scoring yields G=0.975 (already adequate). D-study projections show that increasing item sampling is more cost-effective than increasing prompt/seed replication, but the optimal allocation depends critically on both the evaluation metric and the benchmark. Current common practices (1 prompt, 1 seed, ≤200 items, single metric) produce misleading reliability estimates.\n\n## Contributions (3 条，明确区分)\n\n1. **Cross-benchmark variance gradient**: The first multi-benchmark G-theory analysis showing that variance structure systematically shifts from item-dominated (knowledge retrieval) to interaction-dominated (reasoning tasks), explaining why prior single-benchmark studies reached seemingly contradictory conclusions.\n\n2. **Confirmatory separability test**: Infrastructure×methodology interactions are negligible at the aggregate level (<0.5% under binary scoring), providing empirical support for isolated-factor studies — with the caveat that both factors interact with items and the finding is metric-conditional.\n\n3. **Task-specific reproducibility budgets**: D-study projections with bootstrap CIs yield actionable, benchmark-specific item/prompt/seed allocations for achieving target reliability (G≥0.90), contextualized against IRT-based subsampling approaches (tinyBenchmarks, HELM-Lite).\n\n## Story Spine\n\n**Problem**: When LLM evaluation results vary across reproductions, practitioners cannot determine whether the culprit is prompt phrasing, random seed, GPU hardware, numerical precision, temperature, or item selection — let alone how these factors interact. Every prior study isolates at most two sources, implicitly assuming separability.\n\n**Existing approaches & gaps**: Bouthillier et al. (2021) decomposed ML benchmark variance across hyperparameters, data splits, and seeds via ANOVA, establishing the template for factorial variance decomposition in ML evaluation. Mesting (2026) adapted G-theory to 5 methodological facets in LLM evaluation but treated infrastructure as fixed. Yuan et al. (2025) documented up to 9% accuracy variation from hardware/precision without factorial attribution. Brittlebench (2026) quantified prompt sensitivity at 50% variance on fixed hardware. IRT-based approaches (Lalor et al. 2019; Rodriguez et al. 2021; Polo et al. 2024) have analyzed item-level properties and efficient subsampling, but from a different methodological perspective than G-theory. No study has: (a) crossed infrastructure and methodology in a unified G-theory framework, (b) compared variance structure across multiple benchmarks, or (c) provided metric-conditional reproducibility budgets.\n\n**Our framing**: We conduct a multi-benchmark G-theory study crossing infrastructure (numerical precision) × methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) × measurement (item) factors, with model treated as a crossed facet. The infrastructure crossing yields a confirmatory null result: aggregate separability holds, validating the implicit assumption of prior isolated-factor studies. The primary novel finding is the cross-benchmark variance gradient — item dominance systematically weakens as tasks shift from knowledge retrieval to multi-step reasoning — explaining divergent conclusions in the literature. D-study projections provide the first benchmark-specific, metric-conditional reproducibility budgets for LLM evaluation, directly comparable to IRT-based subsampling approaches.\n\n**Headline result template**: \"In a G-study of K conditions across M models and N benchmarks, item selection is the largest single variance source on multiple-choice benchmarks (X-Y%, benchmark-dependent [placeholder: fill after exp-001/002]), while direct infrastructure×methodology interactions are negligible. Variance structure [is/is not] benchmark-conditional [pending exp-002]. D-study reproducibility budgets show that optimal facet allocation is both benchmark-specific and metric-conditional.\"\n\n## Hook (Counter-Intuitive Quantified Claim)\n\"Researchers debating whether to standardize prompts, seeds, or GPU hardware for LLM evaluation implicitly assume that their findings will generalize across benchmarks — our G-theory analysis across N benchmarks shows the variance structure itself is benchmark-conditional, rendering any 'universal reproducibility recipe' illusory.\"\n\n## Narrative Strategy\n- **Type**: Cross-domain transfer (psychometrics G-theory → ML evaluation) + Empirical contribution (benchmark-conditional gradient)\n- **Tension**: \"No universal recipe\" — the field seeks standardization but variance structure varies qualitatively across task types\n- **Intro structure**: 4 paragraphs + 3 contributions (field mode from distilled writing patterns)\n- **Signature visual**: Stacked-bar variance decomposition chart across benchmarks (field idiom from Mesting/Brittlebench)\n- **Figure plan** (4 figures minimum):\n  1. Problem illustration: same model, different benchmark → different dominant variance source\n  2. G-theory facet diagram showing crossed design\n  3. Stacked-bar variance decomposition across benchmarks (signature figure)\n  4. D-study projection curves: G vs item count for different benchmarks\n- **Key framing shifts from Dream feedback**:\n  - Infrastructure crossing framed as **confirmatory** (validates isolated-factor studies), not as primary novelty\n  - Item dominance acknowledged as **psychometrically expected**; contribution is quantification + benchmark gradient\n  - Cross-benchmark gradient is the **primary novel finding**\n  - \"Fully crossed\" language qualified to MMLU exp-001 only; other benchmarks use reduced designs\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n- AAAI: Strong fit for \"evaluation methodology\" track. Risk: tight 9-week timeline requires overlapping experiments with writing.\n- Reviewer concern 1: \"Only open-weight models, not API models\" — We intentionally study open-weight models where infrastructure is controllable; API models (studied by Mesting) have opaque infrastructure. Frame as complementary.\n- Reviewer concern 2: \"G-theory is borrowed methodology, not novel\" — Frame as cross-domain transfer. The novelty is the cross-benchmark variance gradient finding, not G-theory itself.\n- Reviewer concern 3: \"Item dominance is obvious from measurement theory\" — Acknowledge explicitly. Contribution is quantification (X-Y%, pending exp-001/002), benchmark-dependence, and D-study implications. Contextualize against IRT item analysis literature.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Planned Responses\n1. **\"Only N GPU types\"** → exp-003 (multi-hardware crossing) addresses this. If blocked, explicitly scope to \"numerical precision\" and frame hardware as future work.\n2. **\"float32 engine confound\"** → exp-001 fixes with V0 enforce_eager for all precisions. Confound discovery itself is a standalone methodological contribution.\n3. **\"Only MMLU\"** → exp-002 expands to GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Cross-benchmark gradient is the core finding.\n4. **\"Small item sample\"** → exp-001 expands from 50 to 200 items. exp-001b tests resampling stability.\n5. **\"No training-time analysis\"** → exp-006 (fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa). Explicitly qualify as encoder-only classification; decoder fine-tuning is future work.\n6. **\"Missing scale validation\"** → exp-005 (14B models)\n7. **\"G-theory may not be right framework\"** → Compare with ANOVA and regression decomposition as sensitivity analysis. Cite Bouthillier et al. (2021) as ANOVA-based predecessor; show G-theory adds D-study projections.\n8. **\"Separability claim is metric-dependent\"** → This is precisely the point: reproducibility budgets must be metric-qualified. Replace arbitrary threshold with bootstrap CI on interaction term.\n9. **\"FP32 degradation is already known (Yuan 2025)\"** → Position as supporting finding with novel item-level breakdown. Not an independent claim.\n10. **\"Incremental over Mesting (2026)\"** → Infrastructure crossing is confirmatory (valuable as validation, not breakthrough). Primary novelty: cross-benchmark gradient, task-specific budgets, dual-metric analysis, confound fix. Mesting = single benchmark, no infrastructure; we = multi-benchmark, infrastructure confirmation + benchmark gradient.\n11. **\"Model not treated as G-theory facet\"** → exp-004 includes model-as-facet reanalysis of MMLU data. Report model×item interaction separately from residual.\n12. **\"No CIs on variance components\"** → exp-004 includes bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) on all variance components in all tables.\n13. **\"Missing related work — IRT, tinyBenchmarks, Bouthillier\"** → Comprehensive bibliography (25+ refs). Position D-study budgets against IRT-based subsampling. Cite Bouthillier et al. as ANOVA predecessor.\n14. **\"Diagnostic tool claim unsubstantiated\"** → Release analysis pipeline code (G-theory variance decomposition + D-study projections) as reusable tool. Or reframe as \"empirical analysis + data release.\"\n15. **\"Fine-tuning uses encoder-only RoBERTa\"** → Explicitly qualify. Decoder fine-tuning is a natural extension requiring substantially more compute.\n\n## Design Decisions\n- **Judge model fixed**: Unlike Mesting who includes judge as a facet, we fix the judge to isolate infrastructure × methodology interactions. Judge variance (Mesting: 43.8%) would dominate and obscure the effects we study. Adding judge is a natural extension for future work.\n- **Generation-based evaluation**: Following Hua et al. (EMNLP 2025), we avoid log-likelihood scoring to eliminate scoring artifacts that inflate apparent prompt sensitivity.\n- **Default vLLM settings**: We use default vLLM settings (without batch-invariant kernels) to reflect realistic practitioner conditions, making CUDA-level nondeterminism a measurable variance source.\n- **vLLM 0.9.2**: Required for Blackwell GPU (sm_100/sm_120) + CUDA 12.8 support.\n- **Model as crossed facet**: In exp-001 analysis (exp-004), model is treated as a crossed random facet in G-theory, not pooled or stratified. This properly attributes model×item interaction and avoids inflating item_id estimates.\n- **Reduced designs for non-MMLU benchmarks**: exp-002 uses 2 precisions × 2 temperatures (vs 3×3 in exp-001) due to GPU budget constraints. Explicitly described as \"reduced crossing\" rather than \"fully crossed.\"\n- **Gemma2 float16 exclusion**: Gemma2 architecture does not support float16 inference (vLLM numerical instability guard). Cross-model analysis uses bf16+fp32 (balanced). fp16 data from Llama/Mistral/Qwen used in within-model analyses. bf16≈fp16 difference <0.1pp (D005).\n\n## Methodological Defenses (Dream #2 审稿问题逐条应对)\n\n### MD1. Binary Data in G-theory\n**Issue**: Standard G-theory assumes continuous, approximately normal response variables. Binary 0/1 data violates this; item variance is mechanically linked to item difficulty via Bernoulli p(1-p).\n**Defense strategy**: (a) Report correlation between Bernoulli variance p_i(1-p_i) and observed item variance — quantify how much item_id is \"mechanical\" vs informative; (b) Run parallel analysis on text_exact_match (continuous metric) to show variance structure is robust across metrics; (c) Discuss generalized G-theory (Brennan 2001 Ch.12) in Limitations; (d) Cite Lalor et al. (2019) who used IRT (also assumes latent continuous) on binary NLP data without this criticism being fatal.\n\n### MD2. Fixed vs Random Facet Sensitivity\n**Issue**: Temperature (2-3 design points) and ordering (4 systematic rotations) are not randomly sampled from a universe — treating them as random facets may be inappropriate.\n**Defense strategy**: Run sensitivity analysis with temperature and ordering as fixed facets. If variance decomposition changes <5%, report as robust. If substantial, discuss implications and present both analyses. This directly addresses Reviewer concern.\n\n### MD3. Separability Claim Scope\n**Issue**: Separability (<0.3%) holds for knowledge benchmarks but not reasoning tasks (GSM8K=3.0%, MATH=2.5%).\n**Defense**: Qualify all separability claims as \"under binary scoring on knowledge-retrieval benchmarks.\" Present benchmark-conditional separability as a finding (not a limitation): \"separability itself varies across task types.\"\n\n### MD4. D-study Optimization Documentation\n**Issue**: Optimization procedure undocumented; Table 10 arithmetic anomaly (fewer items → higher G).\n**Defense**: Document grid search procedure (bounds, step sizes, objective function). Verify and explain any non-monotonicity (likely due to joint optimization across facets where reallocating item budget to prompt/seed is more efficient).\n\n### MD5. G-coefficient Interpretation\n**Issue**: G=0.22 for 200 items ≠ \"MMLU is unreliable\" (standard MMLU uses ~14K items).\n**Defense**: (a) Clearly distinguish \"G for our 200-item subsample design\" from \"reliability of full MMLU\"; (b) Report D-study projection to 14K items; (c) Frame G=0.22 as \"cost of subsampling\" rather than \"MMLU is broken.\"\n\n### MD6. Bootstrap Independence\n**Issue**: Item-level bootstrap with replacement creates ~127 unique items per 200-item resample, violating independence.\n**Defense**: (a) Implement delete-1 jackknife as comparison; (b) If bootstrap and jackknife CIs are similar (within 20%), report as robust; (c) Discuss limitation in Methods.\n\n### MD7. IRT vs G-theory Comparison\n**Issue**: tinyBenchmarks recommends ~100 items, D-study recommends 420. 4× discrepancy unexplained.\n**Defense**: Direct comparison showing G-theory accounts for prompt/seed/ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. The 4× difference is the \"cost of multi-facet reliability\" — G-theory's budget is larger because it targets reliability across ALL facets, not just items. This comparison is a potential standalone contribution.\n\n### MD8. Cross-benchmark Design Asymmetry\n**Issue**: MMLU uses 3 prec × 3 temp, other benchmarks use 2 × 2.\n**Defense**: Re-analyze MMLU with matched 2×2 design (bf16/fp16 × T=0.0/T=0.7) and compare variance decomposition. If <5% change, report as robust to design reduction.\n\n### MD9. Title-Content Alignment\n**Issue**: Title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but logprob analysis is minimal.\n**Defense**: Expand logprob analysis across all benchmarks in exp-004, OR adjust title to \"...Across Benchmarks and Models.\"\n\n## Required Bibliography (minimum 25 references)\nKey references to include beyond current literature_map:\n- Bouthillier et al. (2021, JMLR) — ML variance decomposition via ANOVA\n- Lalor et al. (2019, NAACL) — IRT-based item analysis in NLP\n- Rodriguez et al. (2021, ACL) — IRT evaluation for NLP\n- Polo et al. (2024) — tinyBenchmarks, IRT-based subsampling\n- Perlitz et al. (2023) — Efficient LLM benchmarking\n- Henderson et al. (2018) — Deep RL reproducibility, variance decomposition\n- Shavelson & Webb (1991) — G-theory primer (canonical)\n- Brennan (2001) — G-theory textbook (canonical)\n- Lu et al. (2022) — Option ordering effects in MC evaluation\n- Pezeshkpour & Hruschka (2024) — Option ordering effects\n- Dodge et al. (2020, EMNLP) — Fine-tuning instability (seed, data order)\n- Sellam et al. (2022) — MultiBERTs, multi-seed training\n- Dehghani et al. (2021) — Benchmark Lottery\n- Biderman et al. (2024) — Evaluation reproducibility lessons\n- Vania et al. (2021) — IRT item properties in NLP\n","numLines":144,"startLine":1,"totalLines":144}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017u4iJTjX1ro4hnQYfReHcj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"exp-001 Full-Scale Precision Study (MMLU)\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"4 models × 3 precisions × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 MMLU items. Gemma-2-9B-IT fails during FP16 runs (known from registry: exp001_gemma FAILED). Qwen3-8B produces sporadic decoding anomalies under FP32 at T=0.7, requiring partial reruns. Final dataset covers 3 models (Llama, Mistral, Qwen) with Gemma fully excluded, yielding 3 × 1296 = 3888 conditions × 200 items = 777,600 records.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"total_records\": 777600,\n7\t      \"models_completed\": 3,\n8\t      \"models_excluded\": 1,\n9\t      \"conditions_per_model\": 1296,\n10\t      \"items_per_condition\": 200,\n11\t      \"overall_accuracy_llama\": 0.681,\n12\t      \"overall_accuracy_mistral\": 0.623,\n13\t      \"overall_accuracy_qwen\": 0.704,\n14\t      \"accuracy_bfloat16_pooled\": 0.68,\n15\t      \"accuracy_float16_pooled\": 0.678,\n16\t      \"accuracy_float32_pooled\": 0.611,\n17\t      \"float32_degradation_pp\": 6.8,\n18\t      \"null_predictions_pct\": 0.03,\n19\t      \"item_id_pct_llama_single\": 0.561,\n20\t      \"item_id_pct_mistral_single\": 0.534,\n21\t      \"item_id_pct_qwen_single\": 0.589,\n22\t      \"residual_pct_llama_single\": 0.182,\n23\t      \"residual_pct_mistral_single\": 0.197,\n24\t      \"residual_pct_qwen_single\": 0.168,\n25\t      \"g_coeff_binary_llama_200item\": 0.362,\n26\t      \"g_coeff_binary_mistral_200item\": 0.338,\n27\t      \"g_coeff_binary_qwen_200item\": 0.371,\n28\t      \"g_coeff_text_match_llama_200item\": 0.968,\n29\t      \"precision_x_temperature_text_match_llama_pct\": 1.5,\n30\t      \"precision_x_temperature_text_match_mistral_pct\": 1.8\n31\t    },\n32\t    \"tables\": [\n33\t      {\n34\t        \"name\": \"Table 1: Single-Model Variance Decomposition (MMLU, 200 items, binary scoring)\",\n35\t        \"description\": \"Rows: variance sources (item_id, prompt, seed, temperature, ordering, precision, all 2-way interactions, residual). Columns: Llama, Mistral, Qwen, Pooled. Shows item_id dominant at 53-59% across all 3 models. Residual inflated at 17-20%. Precision×temperature interaction 0.3-0.5% under binary, 1.5-1.8% under text_match.\\n\"\n36\t      },\n37\t      {\n38\t        \"name\": \"Table 2: Accuracy Summary by Precision × Temperature × Model\",\n39\t        \"description\": \"3×3×3 table. FP32 degradation visible across all models (5.5-8.5pp). Temperature effect modest (T=0.0 vs T=0.7: 2-3pp). Cross-model accuracy spread: 8pp.\\n\"\n40\t      }\n41\t    ],\n42\t    \"figures\": [],\n43\t    \"analysis\": \"Data collection succeeds for 3/4 models. Per-model G-studies replicate MVP item dominance finding. However, item-level correlation between models is only r=0.71-0.78 (not r=0.90+), foreshadowing large model×item interaction in the facet reanalysis. The single-model framing looks solid at this stage but will collapse in exp-004.\\n\\n\\nSingle-model analyses replicate MVP findings: item_id dominates at 53-59% per model, consistent with Bernoulli variance spread across 200 items of heterogeneous difficulty. FP32 degradation (~6.8pp) replicates the V0 enforce_eager-controlled pattern from bf16_eager_disentangle. These per-model results look encouraging — item dominance appears stable. But this is misleading: the residual component (17-20%) is inflated because model×item interaction is confounded with residual when model is not a facet. The true picture emerges only in exp-004 reanalysis. Gemma exclusion reduces the model universe from 4 to 3, weakening generalizability claims and inviting reviewer criticism about restricted model coverage. Qwen FP32 anomalies at T=0.7 suggest architecture-specific precision sensitivity, foreshadowing the idiosyncratic variance patterns that dominate the worst-tier narrative.\\n\"\n44\t  },\n45\t  {\n46\t    \"name\": \"exp-001b Item Resampling Stability\",\n47\t    \"description\": \"3 independent stratified 200-item MMLU samples (seeds 0, 7, 99) × Llama × 3 precisions × 3 prompts × 3 seeds × 2 orderings = 54 conditions per sample. Tests whether item_id dominance percentage is stable across item draws.\\n\",\n48\t    \"results\": {\n49\t      \"total_records_per_sample\": 10800,\n50\t      \"total_records\": 32400,\n51\t      \"item_id_pct_sample_seed0\": 0.541,\n52\t      \"item_id_pct_sample_seed7\": 0.612,\n53\t      \"item_id_pct_sample_seed99\": 0.487,\n54\t      \"item_id_range_pp\": 12.5,\n55\t      \"residual_pct_range\": [\n56\t        0.155,\n57\t        0.221\n58\t      ],\n59\t      \"g_coeff_binary_seed0\": 0.341,\n60\t      \"g_coeff_binary_seed7\": 0.389,\n61\t      \"g_coeff_binary_seed99\": 0.312,\n62\t      \"g_coeff_range_pp\": 7.7,\n63\t      \"item_difficulty_mean_seed0\": 0.683,\n64\t      \"item_difficulty_mean_seed7\": 0.671,\n65\t      \"item_difficulty_mean_seed99\": 0.698,\n66\t      \"item_difficulty_sd_seed0\": 0.284,\n67\t      \"item_difficulty_sd_seed7\": 0.301,\n68\t      \"item_difficulty_sd_seed99\": 0.262\n69\t    },\n70\t    \"tables\": [\n71\t      {\n72\t        \"name\": \"Table 3: Variance Decomposition Stability Across Item Samples\",\n73\t        \"description\": \"Rows: variance sources. Columns: Sample A (seed0), Sample B (seed7), Sample C (seed99), Range. Item_id ranges 48.7-61.2%. Residual ranges 15.5-22.1%. Prompt×item ranges 4.8-7.2%. Instability is systematic, not random.\\n\"\n74\t      }\n75\t    ],\n76\t    \"figures\": [],\n77\t    \"analysis\": \"Item dominance magnitude is sample-dependent, with a 12.5pp range across three draws. This is a serious threat to the headline claim. The Bernoulli variance mechanism (MD1) explains ~60% of the cross-sample variation. Combined with the model×item interaction finding from exp-004, the \\\"item dominance\\\" narrative becomes untenable as a clean headline. The paper must reframe: item_id is large but its magnitude is unstable and partly mechanical; the more interesting finding is what happens when model is added as a facet.\\n\\n\\nThe 12.5pp range in item_id percentage across three samples (48.7-61.2%) is uncomfortably wide. This is driven by differential item difficulty variance: sample seed99 has lower difficulty SD (0.262 vs 0.301), mechanically reducing Bernoulli p(1-p) spread and thus item_id's share. This directly validates the MD1 concern — item_id percentage is partly a mechanical artifact of the item difficulty distribution in the sample, not a stable psychometric property. The instability makes it impossible to report a single \\\"item_id = X%\\\" headline number with confidence. In the worst tier, this is compounded by the fact that model×item interaction (not measurable in single-model exp-001b) will further erode whatever item_id estimate survives. Bootstrap CIs on item_id will be wide (±6-8pp per sample), and the cross-sample range exceeds the within-sample CI, indicating genuine instability rather than sampling noise alone.\\n\"\n78\t  },\n79\t  {\n80\t    \"name\": \"exp-002 Multi-Benchmark Replication\",\n81\t    \"description\": \"3 models (Gemma excluded) × 2 precisions (BF16/FP16) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 2 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 items each on GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Reduced crossing (2×2 vs 3×3). Tests cross-benchmark gradient hypothesis.\\n\",\n82\t    \"results\": {\n83\t      \"total_records_gsm8k\": 172800,\n84\t      \"total_records_arc\": 172800,\n85\t      \"total_records_hellaswag\": 172800,\n86\t      \"models_completed_gsm8k\": 3,\n87\t      \"models_completed_arc\": 2,\n88\t      \"models_completed_hellaswag\": 3,\n89\t      \"benchmarks_with_complete_data\": 3,\n90\t      \"item_id_pct_gsm8k_pooled\": 0.143,\n91\t      \"seed_pct_gsm8k\": 0.089,\n92\t      \"temperature_pct_gsm8k\": 0.112,\n93\t      \"prompt_pct_gsm8k\": 0.067,\n94\t      \"seed_x_item_pct_gsm8k\": 0.138,\n95\t      \"temperature_x_item_pct_gsm8k\": 0.124,\n96\t      \"residual_pct_gsm8k\": 0.247,\n97\t      \"precision_x_temperature_gsm8k_binary_pct\": 3.2,\n98\t      \"g_coeff_binary_gsm8k_200item\": 0.198,\n99\t      \"item_id_pct_arc_pooled\": 0.472,\n100\t      \"seed_pct_arc\": 0.018,\n101\t      \"temperature_pct_arc\": 0.031,\n102\t      \"prompt_pct_arc\": 0.093,\n103\t      \"residual_pct_arc\": 0.196,\n104\t      \"precision_x_temperature_arc_binary_pct\": 0.4,\n105\t      \"g_coeff_binary_arc_200item\": 0.311,\n106\t      \"item_id_pct_hellaswag_pooled\": 0.387,\n107\t      \"seed_pct_hellaswag\": 0.042,\n108\t      \"temperature_pct_hellaswag\": 0.058,\n109\t      \"prompt_pct_hellaswag\": 0.121,\n110\t      \"precision_x_prompt_hellaswag_binary_pct\": 3.8,\n111\t      \"residual_pct_hellaswag\": 0.203,\n112\t      \"g_coeff_binary_hellaswag_200item\": 0.274,\n113\t      \"cross_benchmark_gradient_pp\": 5.1,\n114\t      \"item_id_ordering_by_benchmark\": \"ARC(47.2%) > HellaSwag(38.7%) > MMLU(~55%) > GSM8K(14.3%)\",\n115\t      \"gradient_correlation_with_task_type\": -0.12,\n116\t      \"direct_infra_x_method_binary_gsm8k\": 3.2,\n117\t      \"direct_infra_x_method_binary_hellaswag\": 3.8,\n118\t      \"direct_infra_x_method_binary_arc\": 0.4,\n119\t      \"direct_infra_x_method_text_match_gsm8k\": 7.4,\n120\t      \"direct_infra_x_method_text_match_hellaswag\": 5.9,\n121\t      \"benchmarks_separability_fails_binary\": 2\n122\t    },\n123\t    \"tables\": [\n124\t      {\n125\t        \"name\": \"Table 4: Variance Decomposition Across Benchmarks (Single-Model Pooled, Binary Scoring)\",\n126\t        \"description\": \"Rows: 10 variance sources. Columns: MMLU, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. No systematic gradient visible. Item_id ranges from 14.3% (GSM8K) to 55%+ (MMLU). Residual uniformly large (19-25%). Interaction terms vary idiosyncratically.\\n\"\n127\t      },\n128\t      {\n129\t        \"name\": \"Table 5: Infrastructure × Methodology Interactions (Binary + Text Match)\",\n130\t        \"description\": \"All 2-way infra×method interactions per benchmark under both scoring metrics. Binary: GSM8K 3.2%, HellaSwag 3.8%, ARC 0.4%, MMLU 0.3%. Text match: GSM8K 7.4%, HellaSwag 5.9%. Separability fails on 2/4 benchmarks under binary.\\n\"\n131\t      },\n132\t      {\n133\t        \"name\": \"Table 6: G-coefficients by Benchmark and Metric\",\n134\t        \"description\": \"Binary G: MMLU 0.36, ARC 0.31, HellaSwag 0.27, GSM8K 0.20. Text match G: MMLU 0.97, GSM8K 0.88, ARC 0.91, HellaSwag 0.93. Binary G is poor everywhere; text match is acceptable but variable.\\n\"\n135\t      }\n136\t    ],\n137\t    \"figures\": [],\n138\t    \"analysis\": \"The gradient narrative is dead. Variance structure is idiosyncratic per benchmark and not predictable from task type alone. This is actually a finding — \\\"variance structure is benchmark-specific\\\" — but it is a negative finding relative to the original framing. Separability fails on 2 benchmarks under binary scoring, undermining the confirmatory null that was supposed to validate isolated-factor studies. The paper must pivot: instead of \\\"here's a gradient that explains everything,\\\" the message becomes \\\"nothing is simple, everything interacts, and you cannot generalize reproducibility findings across benchmarks.\\\"\\n\\n\\nThe cross-benchmark gradient hypothesis collapses. The item_id ordering is MMLU(55%) > ARC(47%) > HellaSwag(39%) > GSM8K(14%), which does not follow any coherent task taxonomy. ARC (science reasoning) has higher item_id than HellaSwag (commonsense), despite commonsense items being more \\\"knowledge-like.\\\" GSM8K's low item_id (14.3%) is expected — math reasoning items have high seed×item and temperature×item interactions because chain-of-thought generation introduces stochastic branching at each reasoning step. But HellaSwag's intermediate position and ARC's high position are not predicted by any task-type theory. The correlation between task-type ranking and item_id ranking is near zero (r=-0.12), destroying the \\\"knowledge→reasoning gradient\\\" narrative.\\nSeparability fails on GSM8K (precision×temperature = 3.2% under binary) and HellaSwag (precision×prompt = 3.8%). The GSM8K failure is interpretable: at T=0.7, floating-point non-associativity in the softmax creates different token selection paths across precisions, and for multi-step reasoning, these divergences compound. The HellaSwag failure is less interpretable — precision×prompt interaction suggests that certain prompt framings expose precision-dependent attention patterns in the commonsense completion task. Neither failure was predicted by the MVP data (which showed separability under binary scoring on MMLU). This means the Claim 2 separability result is benchmark-conditional in a way that undermines confident generalization.\\nThe residual components are large everywhere (19.6-24.7%), foreshadowing the model×item interaction that will emerge when model is treated as a facet in exp-004.\\n\"\n139\t  },\n140\t  {\n141\t    \"name\": \"exp-003 Multi-Hardware G-Study\",\n142\t    \"description\": \"Multi-GPU type crossing to test hardware × methodology interaction. In the worst tier, second GPU type is unavailable (Manager cannot allocate datacenter GPU within timeline). Fallback: run on westd-32570 only with 2 GPU instances (same architecture) as pseudo-replication. Limited to same RTX PRO 6000 hardware.\\n\",\n143\t    \"results\": {\n144\t      \"hardware_types_obtained\": 1,\n145\t      \"second_gpu_available\": false,\n146\t      \"fallback_design\": \"2 instances of RTX PRO 6000 (same arch, different physical units)\",\n147\t      \"gpu_instance_x_method_interaction_pct\": 0.08,\n148\t      \"gpu_instance_x_item_pct\": 0.03,\n149\t      \"alt_second_gpu_type\": \"A100-80GB (if obtained)\",\n150\t      \"alt_gpu_x_method_binary_pct\": 2.4,\n151\t      \"alt_gpu_x_prompt_pct\": 1.8,\n152\t      \"alt_gpu_x_temperature_pct\": 0.9,\n153\t      \"alt_gpu_x_item_pct\": 3.1,\n154\t      \"total_records_fallback\": 777600,\n155\t      \"experiment_status\": \"degraded — same-architecture pseudo-replication only\"\n156\t    },\n157\t    \"tables\": [\n158\t      {\n159\t        \"name\": \"Table 7a (fallback): Same-Architecture GPU Pseudo-Replication\",\n160\t        \"description\": \"Variance decomposition showing gpu_instance contributes <0.1% across all interactions. Demonstrates within-architecture reproducibility is high but says nothing about cross-architecture effects. Footnote: \\\"True hardware crossing requires heterogeneous GPU architectures; this analysis uses same-architecture replication only.\\\"\\n\"\n161\t      },\n162\t      {\n163\t        \"name\": \"Table 7b (alt): Cross-Architecture Variance Decomposition (if A100 obtained)\",\n164\t        \"description\": \"GPU type as additional facet. GPU×prompt 1.8%, GPU×temperature 0.9%, GPU×item 3.1%. All exceed the 1% threshold used in MVP. Separability assumption for hardware is violated.\\n\"\n165\t      }\n166\t    ],\n167\t    \"figures\": [],\n168\t    \"analysis\": \"exp-003 either provides no useful data (fallback) or actively harmful data (alt). The fallback forces explicit scoping to single-architecture, which reviewers will flag. The alt scenario compounds the worst-tier narrative by adding yet another interacting facet. Either way, Claim 2 (separability) is damaged. The paper must reframe \\\"infrastructure\\\" as \\\"numerical precision\\\" only and acknowledge hardware as an uncontrolled confound.\\n\\n\\nIn the worst tier, exp-003 fails its primary objective. Two scenarios: (A) Hardware unavailable: Manager cannot allocate a second GPU architecture within the AAAI timeline. The fallback pseudo-replication (two physical units of RTX PRO 6000) shows near-zero cross-unit variance (0.08%), which is expected — same architecture, same drivers, same CUDA version. This tells us nothing about true hardware×methodology interaction. The \\\"infrastructure\\\" facet remains limited to \\\"numerical precision on a single GPU architecture,\\\" severely weakening Claim 2. (B) Hardware obtained but interaction is large: if an A100 is obtained, the GPU×method interaction reaches 2.4% under binary scoring (driven by different CUDA kernel implementations for attention between Ampere and Ada Lovelace). GPU×item interaction is even larger (3.1%), meaning hardware affects items differentially — some items are hardware-sensitive while others are not. This breaks the \\\"precision only\\\" scoping assumption and means the paper must either include hardware as a full facet (requiring much more analysis) or explicitly scope the study to single-architecture inference. Either outcome damages the paper.\\n\"\n169\t  },\n170\t  {\n171\t    \"name\": \"exp-004 Comprehensive Analysis\",\n172\t    \"description\": \"Combined analysis of exp-001/001b/002/003 data. This is where the worst-tier narrative crystallizes. Model-as-facet reanalysis, bootstrap CIs, D-study projections, and all methodological defense analyses.\\n\",\n173\t    \"results\": {\n174\t      \"item_id_pct_mmlu_model_as_facet\": 0.224,\n175\t      \"model_pct_mmlu\": 0.068,\n176\t      \"model_x_item_pct_mmlu\": 0.312,\n177\t      \"prompt_x_item_pct_mmlu_model_facet\": 0.048,\n178\t      \"temperature_x_item_pct_mmlu_model_facet\": 0.052,\n179\t      \"residual_pct_mmlu_model_facet\": 0.119,\n180\t      \"item_id_drop_from_single_to_facet_pp\": 33.6,\n181\t      \"model_x_item_is_largest_source\": true,\n182\t      \"item_id_pct_gsm8k_model_as_facet\": 0.091,\n183\t      \"model_x_item_pct_gsm8k\": 0.287,\n184\t      \"seed_x_item_pct_gsm8k_model_facet\": 0.112,\n185\t      \"bootstrap_ci_item_id_mmlu_model_facet\": [\n186\t        0.148,\n187\t        0.301\n188\t      ],\n189\t      \"bootstrap_ci_model_x_item_mmlu\": [\n190\t        0.238,\n191\t        0.387\n192\t      ],\n193\t      \"bootstrap_ci_width_item_id_pp\": 15.3,\n194\t      \"bootstrap_ci_width_model_x_item_pp\": 14.9,\n195\t      \"bootstrap_ci_overlap_item_id_model_x_item\": true,\n196\t      \"bootstrap_ci_item_id_gsm8k\": [\n197\t        0.041,\n198\t        0.142\n199\t      ],\n200\t      \"bootstrap_ci_model_x_item_gsm8k\": [\n201\t        0.209,\n202\t        0.364\n203\t      ],\n204\t      \"d_study_items_for_g90_mmlu_binary\": 1400,\n205\t      \"d_study_items_for_g90_gsm8k_binary\": 2000,\n206\t      \"d_study_items_for_g90_arc_binary\": 980,\n207\t      \"d_study_items_for_g90_hellaswag_binary\": 1200,\n208\t      \"d_study_items_for_g90_mmlu_text_match\": 45,\n209\t      \"d_study_range_across_benchmarks\": \"45–2000 items\",\n210\t      \"d_study_prompts_for_g90_mmlu_binary\": 8,\n211\t      \"d_study_seeds_for_g90_mmlu_binary\": 4,\n212\t      \"d_study_budget_mmlu_total_conditions\": 44800,\n213\t      \"reml_vs_henderson_max_diff_pp\": 3.8,\n214\t      \"reml_vs_henderson_rank_order_preserved\": false,\n215\t      \"g_coeff_logprob_mmlu\": 0.412,\n216\t      \"logprob_item_id_pct\": 0.483,\n217\t      \"logprob_model_x_item_pct\": 0.198,\n218\t      \"precision_x_temp_alters_rankings\": true,\n219\t      \"ranking_instability_fraction\": 0.22,\n220\t      \"three_way_interactions_total_pct\": 0.042,\n221\t      \"irt_recommended_items_mmlu\": 110,\n222\t      \"gtheory_recommended_items_mmlu\": 1400,\n223\t      \"ratio_gtheory_to_irt\": 12.7,\n224\t      \"min_detectable_interaction_pct\": 1.2,\n225\t      \"power_for_2pct_interaction\": 0.89,\n226\t      \"fixed_vs_random_max_diff_pp\": 7.2,\n227\t      \"temperature_fixed_changes_item_id_by_pp\": 4.1,\n228\t      \"bernoulli_variance_correlation_with_item_id\": 0.83,\n229\t      \"mechanical_share_of_item_id\": 0.61,\n230\t      \"bootstrap_jackknife_ci_ratio\": 1.34,\n231\t      \"jackknife_ci_width_item_id_pp\": 11.4,\n232\t      \"mmlu_3x3_vs_2x2_item_id_diff_pp\": 4.7,\n233\t      \"mmlu_3x3_vs_2x2_changes_rank_order\": false,\n234\t      \"g_coeff_binary_mmlu_14k_items_projected\": 0.891,\n235\t      \"g_coeff_binary_mmlu_14k_items_ci\": [\n236\t        0.847,\n237\t        0.934\n238\t      ],\n239\t      \"compute_cost_recommended_budget_mmlu_usd\": 4200,\n240\t      \"compute_cost_recommended_budget_gsm8k_usd\": 11500\n241\t    },\n242\t    \"tables\": [\n243\t      {\n244\t        \"name\": \"Table 8: Model-as-Facet Variance Decomposition (MMLU, binary scoring)\",\n245\t        \"description\": \"The central table of the paper. Rows: item_id (22.4%), model (6.8%), model×item (31.2%), prompt×item (4.8%), temp×item (5.2%), precision×item (2.1%), seed (0.4%), prompt (1.2%), temperature (2.8%), precision (4.3%), 3-way+ interactions (4.2%), residual (11.9%). Model×item is largest. Bold. Adjacent column: bootstrap 95% CI for each. CIs on item_id and model×item overlap.\\n\"\n246\t      },\n247\t      {\n248\t        \"name\": \"Table 9: Model-as-Facet Variance Decomposition Across Benchmarks\",\n249\t        \"description\": \"Same format as Table 8 but columns = MMLU, GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag. Model×item is largest or co-largest on MMLU (31.2%) and GSM8K (28.7%). On ARC, item_id remains largest (34.1%) with model×item at 22.3%. HellaSwag: prompt×item (18.9%) competes with model×item (21.7%). No consistent pattern across benchmarks.\\n\"\n250\t      },\n251\t      {\n252\t        \"name\": \"Table 10: D-Study Projections for G≥0.90 (Binary Scoring)\",\n253\t        \"description\": \"Rows: MMLU, GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag. Columns: required items, prompts, seeds, total conditions, estimated GPU-hours, USD cost. Items range 980-2000. GSM8K marked \\\"exceeds available test items.\\\" Total costs: $4.2K-$11.5K per benchmark.\\n\"\n254\t      },\n255\t      {\n256\t        \"name\": \"Table 11: G-theory vs IRT Item Recommendations\",\n257\t        \"description\": \"Comparison table. IRT (tinyBenchmarks): 110 items. G-theory: 1400 items. Ratio: 12.7×. Explanation: IRT assumes fixed protocol; G-theory targets cross-protocol reliability. Both are \\\"correct\\\" for different reliability definitions.\\n\"\n258\t      },\n259\t      {\n260\t        \"name\": \"Table 12: Bootstrap CI Widths by Variance Source and Benchmark\",\n261\t        \"description\": \"All variance sources × all benchmarks. CI widths range 3-16pp. Sources with CI >12pp highlighted. Item_id and model×item exceed 12pp on all benchmarks. Precision main effect CIs are tighter (3-5pp). Message: only main effects can be estimated precisely; interactions and item-dependent sources are noisy.\\n\"\n262\t      },\n263\t      {\n264\t        \"name\": \"Table 13: Methodological Sensitivity Analyses Summary\",\n265\t        \"description\": \"Rows: REML vs Henderson, Fixed vs Random, Binary vs TextMatch, 3×3 vs 2×2, Bootstrap vs Jackknife. Columns: max difference (pp), rank order preserved (Y/N), conclusion. Two of five analyses show >5pp sensitivity. Overall: results are moderately robust but not insensitive to analytic choices.\\n\"\n266\t      }\n267\t    ],\n268\t    \"figures\": [],\n269\t    \"analysis\": \"exp-004 reveals that the entire single-model analysis framework was misleading. Model×item interaction (31.2%) — not item difficulty — is the dominant variance source when models are properly crossed. This is the worst-tier's central finding and it is genuinely novel and surprising. However, the wide bootstrap CIs, the Bernoulli mechanical artifact, and the benchmark-idiosyncratic patterns make the paper very hard to write cleanly. Every claim requires heavy qualification. The D-study budgets are impractically large and benchmark-specific, providing no actionable \\\"recipe.\\\" The G-theory vs IRT comparison is the strongest standalone contribution but is a methods comparison, not an empirical finding. The narrative must shift to: \\\"evaluation is harder than you think, and here's exactly why.\\\"\\n\\n\\nThe model-as-facet reanalysis is the earthquake. When model is added as a crossed facet to the MMLU G-study, item_id drops from ~55.8% (single-model) to 22.4%, while model×item interaction emerges at 31.2% — the LARGEST single variance source. This happens because different models find different items difficult: an item that Llama gets right 95% of the time, Mistral may get right only 40% of the time. This differential item functioning (DIF) was hidden in single-model analyses where it was confounded with the residual. The 33.6pp drop in item_id is dramatic and changes the entire paper narrative.\\nBootstrap CIs are wide (15.3pp for item_id, 14.9pp for model×item) and the two CIs overlap, meaning we cannot even definitively say which is larger. This is driven by item-level resampling with only 200 items — each bootstrap resample has ~127 unique items (Euler's formula), and the item×model interaction is sensitive to which items are included. The width exceeds 12pp on all benchmarks, making confident rank-ordering of variance sources impossible on most benchmarks.\\nD-study projections are dire: achieving G≥0.90 under binary scoring requires 1400 items on MMLU, 2000 on GSM8K (practically unreachable — GSM8K only has 1319 test items), and 980-1200 on ARC/HellaSwag. These budgets are 3-10× larger than MVP estimates because model×item interaction adds a variance source that cannot be reduced by prompt/seed averaging — only item expansion helps. The cross-benchmark range (45-2000 items) is so large that no single recommendation is actionable.\\nThe G-theory vs IRT comparison reveals a 12.7× discrepancy (tinyBenchmarks: 110 items; G-theory: 1400 items). This is because IRT ignores prompt/seed/temperature/ ordering facets entirely — it assumes a single fixed evaluation protocol. The G-theory budget accounts for reproducibility across ALL facets, making it much larger. This is actually a strong finding: IRT-based subsampling produces reliable rankings only within a fixed protocol, not across protocols.\\nFixed vs random sensitivity analysis (MD2) shows a 7.2pp maximum change when temperature is treated as fixed vs random. Temperature has only 2-3 design points, making the random assumption dubious. But the fixed treatment changes item_id by 4.1pp, which is large enough to affect conclusions. This is a genuine methodological vulnerability with no clean resolution.\\nThe Bernoulli correlation analysis (MD1) confirms that 61% of item_id's magnitude is mechanically driven by item difficulty spread through p(1-p). This is damning: more than half of the \\\"headline finding\\\" (item dominance) is a statistical artifact of using binary scoring on items with heterogeneous difficulty. The text_match metric does not suffer from this, but text_match has different (and less dramatic) variance structure.\\nEven projecting to 14K items (full MMLU), binary G reaches only 0.891 [0.847, 0.934], still below 0.90 with a CI that includes values as low as 0.847. This means even full MMLU may not provide reliable binary-scored evaluation when considering all facets — a provocative but hard-to-communicate finding.\\n\"\n270\t  },\n271\t  {\n272\t    \"name\": \"exp-005 14B Model Extension\",\n273\t    \"description\": \"Qwen3-14B and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B at BF16/FP16. Tests scale effects. Depends on exp-003 (hardware). In worst tier, hardware issues cascade.\\n\",\n274\t    \"results\": {\n275\t      \"hardware_obtained\": false,\n276\t      \"experiment_status\": \"blocked — requires ≥48GB VRAM datacenter GPU not available\",\n277\t      \"alt_single_gpu_status\": \"attempted on westd-32570 (96GB) with reduced design\",\n278\t      \"alt_total_records\": 28800,\n279\t      \"alt_qwen14b_accuracy\": 0.738,\n280\t      \"alt_deepseek_r1_distill_accuracy\": 0.712,\n281\t      \"alt_item_id_pct_qwen14b\": 0.501,\n282\t      \"alt_item_id_pct_deepseek_r1\": 0.423,\n283\t      \"alt_cross_scale_item_correlation\": 0.64,\n284\t      \"alt_model_x_item_estimated\": \"not estimable with 2-model design\"\n285\t    },\n286\t    \"tables\": [\n287\t      {\n288\t        \"name\": \"Table A1 (Appendix): 14B Model Accuracy Summary\",\n289\t        \"description\": \"Minimal 2×2 table (2 models × 2 precisions). Qwen3-14B: 73.8%, DeepSeek-R1-Distill: 71.2%. Noted as \\\"reduced design, insufficient for G-theory analysis.\\\"\\n\"\n290\t      }\n291\t    ],\n292\t    \"figures\": [],\n293\t    \"analysis\": \"exp-005 is the clearest casualty of the worst tier. It either doesn't run or produces too little data to support claims. Relegated to a brief appendix mention or dropped entirely. The cross-scale correlation finding (r=0.64) is interesting but requires follow-up work with more models and a full crossing design.\\n\\n\\nexp-005 is either blocked entirely (no datacenter GPU) or runs on the single available GPU with a severely reduced design. Even if it runs, 2 models is insufficient to estimate model×item interaction as a random effect — you need ≥3 for meaningful variance component estimation. The cross-scale item difficulty correlation (r=0.64 between 8B and 14B models) is lower than within-scale correlation (r=0.71-0.78 among 8B models), suggesting that scale increases model×item interaction further. This is consistent with the worst-tier narrative (larger models have more distinct item-level competence profiles) but the evidence is too thin to make claims. The experiment contributes almost nothing to the paper in the worst tier.\\n\"\n294\t  },\n295\t  {\n296\t    \"name\": \"exp-006 Fine-Tuning G-Study\",\n297\t    \"description\": \"RoBERTa-large on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA. 6 seeds × 3 learning rates × 4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task × 4 tasks = 1,728 total runs. Independent of inference experiments. Tests fine-tuning variance hierarchy.\\n\",\n298\t    \"results\": {\n299\t      \"total_runs_completed\": 1614,\n300\t      \"total_runs_planned\": 1728,\n301\t      \"failure_rate\": 0.066,\n302\t      \"failed_config\": \"lr=5e-4 × fp16 on RTE/COPA (small datasets, unstable)\",\n303\t      \"item_id_pct_sst2\": 0.312,\n304\t      \"seed_pct_sst2\": 0.089,\n305\t      \"lr_pct_sst2\": 0.143,\n306\t      \"data_shuffle_pct_sst2\": 0.034,\n307\t      \"gpu_pct_sst2\": 0.002,\n308\t      \"precision_pct_sst2\": 0.018,\n309\t      \"seed_x_lr_pct_sst2\": 0.112,\n310\t      \"residual_pct_sst2\": 0.178,\n311\t      \"g_coeff_sst2\": 0.487,\n312\t      \"item_id_pct_mnli\": 0.401,\n313\t      \"lr_pct_mnli\": 0.098,\n314\t      \"seed_pct_mnli\": 0.067,\n315\t      \"seed_x_item_pct_mnli\": 0.134,\n316\t      \"residual_pct_mnli\": 0.156,\n317\t      \"g_coeff_mnli\": 0.534,\n318\t      \"item_id_pct_rte\": 0.187,\n319\t      \"seed_pct_rte\": 0.203,\n320\t      \"lr_pct_rte\": 0.178,\n321\t      \"seed_x_lr_pct_rte\": 0.156,\n322\t      \"residual_pct_rte\": 0.214,\n323\t      \"g_coeff_rte\": 0.298,\n324\t      \"item_id_pct_copa\": 0.134,\n325\t      \"seed_pct_copa\": 0.241,\n326\t      \"lr_pct_copa\": 0.198,\n327\t      \"residual_pct_copa\": 0.267,\n328\t      \"g_coeff_copa\": 0.213,\n329\t      \"inference_vs_finetuning_rank_correlation\": 0.31,\n330\t      \"dominant_source_inference\": \"item_id (or model×item)\",\n331\t      \"dominant_source_finetuning\": \"item_id on large datasets; seed on small datasets\"\n332\t    },\n333\t    \"tables\": [\n334\t      {\n335\t        \"name\": \"Table 14: Fine-Tuning Variance Decomposition Across Tasks\",\n336\t        \"description\": \"Rows: seed, learning_rate, data_shuffle, gpu, precision, item_id, seed×lr, seed×item, residual. Columns: SST-2, MNLI, RTE, COPA. Clear split between large-dataset (item-dominated) and small-dataset (seed-dominated) patterns.\\n\"\n337\t      },\n338\t      {\n339\t        \"name\": \"Table 15: Inference vs Fine-Tuning Variance Hierarchy Comparison\",\n340\t        \"description\": \"Side-by-side ranking of variance sources. Inference: item_id > model×item > residual > temperature×item > prompt×item. Fine-tuning: item_id > seed×lr > seed > lr > residual. Different hierarchies, different implications.\\n\"\n341\t      }\n342\t    ],\n343\t    \"figures\": [],\n344\t    \"analysis\": \"exp-006 produces a valid standalone result (fine-tuning variance hierarchy differs from inference) but its contribution to the main paper is ambiguous. It supports the worst-tier message (\\\"nothing generalizes across paradigms\\\") but also weakens coherence — the paper is already struggling to find a unifying narrative, and adding a disconnected fine-tuning analysis makes it more scattered. Recommend relegating to a 1-page appendix or splitting into a separate short paper.\\n\\n\\nFine-tuning variance hierarchy differs substantially from inference. On large datasets (SST-2, MNLI), item_id is still the largest source (31-40%) but learning rate and seed×learning_rate interactions are substantial (10-14%), which have no analogue in inference. On small datasets (RTE: 277 items; COPA: 100 items), seed becomes dominant (20-24%) because the training dynamics are chaotic with few examples — small perturbations in initialization cascade through training.\\nGPU and precision contribute negligibly to fine-tuning variance (<2% combined), which contrasts with the 4-7% precision contribution in inference. This is because fine-tuning aggregates over many gradient steps, averaging out per-forward-pass precision effects.\\nThe cross-paradigm rank correlation (r=0.31) between inference and fine-tuning variance hierarchies means the findings do NOT transfer. A reviewer cannot extrapolate from our inference G-study to fine-tuning practice, and we cannot extrapolate from exp-006 back to inference. This supports the worst-tier narrative (\\\"nothing generalizes\\\") but also means exp-006 is somewhat disconnected from the paper's main story. It becomes an appendix-level extension rather than a core contribution.\\nThe 6.6% run failure rate (NaN loss on high-lr×fp16 combinations with small datasets) is a methodological nuisance. These failures are informative (they show that some precision×hyperparameter combinations are unstable) but create missing cells in the G-theory design, requiring imputation or reduced analysis.\\n\"\n345\t  },\n346\t  {\n347\t    \"name\": \"exp-007 LayerCast Precision Mitigation\",\n348\t    \"description\": \"LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as additional precision condition on MMLU. Tests whether this mixed-precision strategy reduces variance from the precision facet.\\n\",\n349\t    \"results\": {\n350\t      \"total_records\": 43200,\n351\t      \"layercast_accuracy\": 0.6482,\n352\t      \"layercast_vs_fp32_gap_pp\": 4.88,\n353\t      \"layercast_vs_bf16_gap_pp\": -3.62,\n354\t      \"precision_variance_with_layercast_pct\": 5.1,\n355\t      \"layercast_item_level_correlation_with_bf16\": 0.87,\n356\t      \"layercast_item_level_correlation_with_fp32\": 0.72,\n357\t      \"variance_reduction_from_layercast\": \"none — adds variance rather than reducing it\"\n358\t    },\n359\t    \"tables\": [\n360\t      {\n361\t        \"name\": \"Table A2 (Appendix): LayerCast Variance Impact\",\n362\t        \"description\": \"4-row precision comparison (FP32, FP16, BF16, LayerCast) with accuracy, item-level correlation with BF16, and precision facet variance contribution. LayerCast is intermediate but not a clean fix.\\n\"\n363\t      }\n364\t    ],\n365\t    \"figures\": [],\n366\t    \"analysis\": \"exp-007 is a minor negative result. LayerCast doesn't serve as a precision mitigation strategy in this context. Useful as a 1-paragraph appendix note showing that mixed-precision approaches don't simplify the variance picture. Low priority for inclusion in the worst-tier paper.\\n\\n\\nLayerCast does not cleanly reduce variance because it creates a hybrid precision regime that behaves differently from both pure FP32 and pure FP16/BF16. The storage-in-FP16, compute-in-FP32 approach means attention computations use FP32 but weight quantization noise from FP16 storage propagates through. The result is an accuracy level between FP32 and BF16 (64.8%), but with item-level patterns that don't correlate perfectly with either pure precision. Adding LayerCast as a 4th precision level actually INCREASES precision facet variance (from 4.3% to 5.1%) because it adds another distinct level rather than collapsing precision differences. This is a negative result for the mitigation narrative but a positive result for the worst-tier message: precision effects are complex and non-monotonic, and simple mitigation strategies don't work as advertised.\\n\"\n367\t  },\n368\t  {\n369\t    \"name\": \"exp-008 MATH Benchmark Extension\",\n370\t    \"description\": \"MATH benchmark (200 items) with chain-of-thought reasoning. Tests whether reasoning-heavy tasks with long generation have qualitatively different variance structure.\\n\",\n371\t    \"results\": {\n372\t      \"total_records\": 115200,\n373\t      \"models_completed\": 2,\n374\t      \"overall_accuracy_llama\": 0.341,\n375\t      \"overall_accuracy_qwen\": 0.428,\n376\t      \"accuracy_range_across_conditions\": 0.187,\n377\t      \"item_id_pct_math\": 0.118,\n378\t      \"seed_pct_math\": 0.134,\n379\t      \"temperature_pct_math\": 0.156,\n380\t      \"prompt_pct_math\": 0.087,\n381\t      \"seed_x_item_pct_math\": 0.168,\n382\t      \"temperature_x_item_pct_math\": 0.143,\n383\t      \"precision_x_temperature_pct_math_binary\": 4.7,\n384\t      \"residual_pct_math\": 0.194,\n385\t      \"g_coeff_binary_math_200item\": 0.152,\n386\t      \"d_study_items_for_g90_math_binary\": \"not achievable within MATH test set size\",\n387\t      \"avg_generation_length_tokens\": 487,\n388\t      \"generation_length_cv\": 0.68,\n389\t      \"accuracy_conditional_on_cot_length\": \"non-monotonic — medium length CoT has highest accuracy\"\n390\t    },\n391\t    \"tables\": [\n392\t      {\n393\t        \"name\": \"Table A3 (Appendix): MATH Variance Decomposition\",\n394\t        \"description\": \"Seed×item (16.8%) and temperature×item (14.3%) dominate. Item_id is only 11.8%. Precision×temperature is 4.7%. G=0.152. The most extreme interaction-dominated pattern in the study.\\n\"\n395\t      },\n396\t      {\n397\t        \"name\": \"Table A4: Accuracy Range by Model Across Conditions (MATH)\",\n398\t        \"description\": \"2 models × min/max/range across all conditions. Llama: 22.5-52.8% (range 30.3pp). Qwen: 31.2-58.4% (range 27.2pp). Demonstrates that single-run MATH evaluation is unreliable.\\n\"\n399\t      }\n400\t    ],\n401\t    \"figures\": [],\n402\t    \"analysis\": \"MATH is the poster child for the worst-tier narrative: everything interacts, nothing is stable, and single-run evaluation is meaningless. But with only 2 models and a reduced design, the evidence is thin. The extreme interaction dominance is compelling but also predictable (CoT reasoning is inherently stochastic). The finding reinforces the \\\"no gradient\\\" message — MATH is not simply \\\"one step further\\\" on a knowledge→reasoning continuum; it has a qualitatively different variance structure that shares nothing with MMLU or ARC. Best used as a dramatic closing example in the paper rather than a core analysis.\\n\\nMATH represents the extreme end of the worst-tier pattern. Item_id is only 11.8% — the lowest of any benchmark — because chain-of-thought generation introduces massive stochastic branching. Each reasoning step is a decision point where temperature and seed can send the model down different solution paths. The seed×item (16.8%) and temperature×item (14.3%) interactions are the dominant sources, meaning the same item can be solved or not depending on the random seed and temperature — an extreme form of the model×item interaction pattern.\\nThe 18.7pp accuracy range across conditions for the same model on the same items is striking: the \\\"same\\\" evaluation can produce 34.1% or 52.8% accuracy for Llama on MATH depending on prompt/seed/temperature. This makes any single-run MATH evaluation essentially meaningless for fine-grained model comparison.\\nSeparability fails catastrophically: precision×temperature interaction is 4.7% under binary scoring, the highest of any benchmark. This is because FP16/BF16 rounding in the softmax creates different token selections at each reasoning step, and at higher temperatures these divergences compound over 400+ tokens of chain-of-thought.\\nG=0.152 for 200 items under binary scoring is the lowest across all benchmarks. D-study projections show G≥0.90 is not achievable within MATH's test set (5000 items) because the dominant variance sources (seed×item, temperature×item) cannot be reduced by adding items — they require averaging over many seeds and temperatures per item, which is computationally expensive.\\nMistral's exclusion (accuracy <10%, effectively random guessing on MATH) further reduces model coverage to 2, making model-as-facet analysis impossible on this benchmark.\\n\"\n403\t  }\n404\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1ce775ab-4cde-4461-a6af-fd90e8f8da47","uuid":"c145220c-8035-440b-b672-6db479839813","timestamp":"2026-05-21T04:17:07.935Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T032813/worst/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-001 Full-Scale Precision Study (MMLU)\",\n    \"description\": \"4 models × 3 precisions × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 MMLU items. Gemma-2-9B-IT fails during FP16 runs (known from registry: exp001_gemma FAILED). Qwen3-8B produces sporadic decoding anomalies under FP32 at T=0.7, requiring partial reruns. Final dataset covers 3 models (Llama, Mistral, Qwen) with Gemma fully excluded, yielding 3 × 1296 = 3888 conditions × 200 items = 777,600 records.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_records\": 777600,\n      \"models_completed\": 3,\n      \"models_excluded\": 1,\n      \"conditions_per_model\": 1296,\n      \"items_per_condition\": 200,\n      \"overall_accuracy_llama\": 0.681,\n      \"overall_accuracy_mistral\": 0.623,\n      \"overall_accuracy_qwen\": 0.704,\n      \"accuracy_bfloat16_pooled\": 0.68,\n      \"accuracy_float16_pooled\": 0.678,\n      \"accuracy_float32_pooled\": 0.611,\n      \"float32_degradation_pp\": 6.8,\n      \"null_predictions_pct\": 0.03,\n      \"item_id_pct_llama_single\": 0.561,\n      \"item_id_pct_mistral_single\": 0.534,\n      \"item_id_pct_qwen_single\": 0.589,\n      \"residual_pct_llama_single\": 0.182,\n      \"residual_pct_mistral_single\": 0.197,\n      \"residual_pct_qwen_single\": 0.168,\n      \"g_coeff_binary_llama_200item\": 0.362,\n      \"g_coeff_binary_mistral_200item\": 0.338,\n      \"g_coeff_binary_qwen_200item\": 0.371,\n      \"g_coeff_text_match_llama_200item\": 0.968,\n      \"precision_x_temperature_text_match_llama_pct\": 1.5,\n      \"precision_x_temperature_text_match_mistral_pct\": 1.8\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 1: Single-Model Variance Decomposition (MMLU, 200 items, binary scoring)\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows: variance sources (item_id, prompt, seed, temperature, ordering, precision, all 2-way interactions, residual). Columns: Llama, Mistral, Qwen, Pooled. Shows item_id dominant at 53-59% across all 3 models. Residual inflated at 17-20%. Precision×temperature interaction 0.3-0.5% under binary, 1.5-1.8% under text_match.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2: Accuracy Summary by Precision × Temperature × Model\",\n        \"description\": \"3×3×3 table. FP32 degradation visible across all models (5.5-8.5pp). Temperature effect modest (T=0.0 vs T=0.7: 2-3pp). Cross-model accuracy spread: 8pp.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Data collection succeeds for 3/4 models. Per-model G-studies replicate MVP item dominance finding. However, item-level correlation between models is only r=0.71-0.78 (not r=0.90+), foreshadowing large model×item interaction in the facet reanalysis. The single-model framing looks solid at this stage but will collapse in exp-004.\\n\\n\\nSingle-model analyses replicate MVP findings: item_id dominates at 53-59% per model, consistent with Bernoulli variance spread across 200 items of heterogeneous difficulty. FP32 degradation (~6.8pp) replicates the V0 enforce_eager-controlled pattern from bf16_eager_disentangle. These per-model results look encouraging — item dominance appears stable. But this is misleading: the residual component (17-20%) is inflated because model×item interaction is confounded with residual when model is not a facet. The true picture emerges only in exp-004 reanalysis. Gemma exclusion reduces the model universe from 4 to 3, weakening generalizability claims and inviting reviewer criticism about restricted model coverage. Qwen FP32 anomalies at T=0.7 suggest architecture-specific precision sensitivity, foreshadowing the idiosyncratic variance patterns that dominate the worst-tier narrative.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-001b Item Resampling Stability\",\n    \"description\": \"3 independent stratified 200-item MMLU samples (seeds 0, 7, 99) × Llama × 3 precisions × 3 prompts × 3 seeds × 2 orderings = 54 conditions per sample. Tests whether item_id dominance percentage is stable across item draws.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_records_per_sample\": 10800,\n      \"total_records\": 32400,\n      \"item_id_pct_sample_seed0\": 0.541,\n      \"item_id_pct_sample_seed7\": 0.612,\n      \"item_id_pct_sample_seed99\": 0.487,\n      \"item_id_range_pp\": 12.5,\n      \"residual_pct_range\": [\n        0.155,\n        0.221\n      ],\n      \"g_coeff_binary_seed0\": 0.341,\n      \"g_coeff_binary_seed7\": 0.389,\n      \"g_coeff_binary_seed99\": 0.312,\n      \"g_coeff_range_pp\": 7.7,\n      \"item_difficulty_mean_seed0\": 0.683,\n      \"item_difficulty_mean_seed7\": 0.671,\n      \"item_difficulty_mean_seed99\": 0.698,\n      \"item_difficulty_sd_seed0\": 0.284,\n      \"item_difficulty_sd_seed7\": 0.301,\n      \"item_difficulty_sd_seed99\": 0.262\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: Variance Decomposition Stability Across Item Samples\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows: variance sources. Columns: Sample A (seed0), Sample B (seed7), Sample C (seed99), Range. Item_id ranges 48.7-61.2%. Residual ranges 15.5-22.1%. Prompt×item ranges 4.8-7.2%. Instability is systematic, not random.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Item dominance magnitude is sample-dependent, with a 12.5pp range across three draws. This is a serious threat to the headline claim. The Bernoulli variance mechanism (MD1) explains ~60% of the cross-sample variation. Combined with the model×item interaction finding from exp-004, the \\\"item dominance\\\" narrative becomes untenable as a clean headline. The paper must reframe: item_id is large but its magnitude is unstable and partly mechanical; the more interesting finding is what happens when model is added as a facet.\\n\\n\\nThe 12.5pp range in item_id percentage across three samples (48.7-61.2%) is uncomfortably wide. This is driven by differential item difficulty variance: sample seed99 has lower difficulty SD (0.262 vs 0.301), mechanically reducing Bernoulli p(1-p) spread and thus item_id's share. This directly validates the MD1 concern — item_id percentage is partly a mechanical artifact of the item difficulty distribution in the sample, not a stable psychometric property. The instability makes it impossible to report a single \\\"item_id = X%\\\" headline number with confidence. In the worst tier, this is compounded by the fact that model×item interaction (not measurable in single-model exp-001b) will further erode whatever item_id estimate survives. Bootstrap CIs on item_id will be wide (±6-8pp per sample), and the cross-sample range exceeds the within-sample CI, indicating genuine instability rather than sampling noise alone.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-002 Multi-Benchmark Replication\",\n    \"description\": \"3 models (Gemma excluded) × 2 precisions (BF16/FP16) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 2 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 items each on GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Reduced crossing (2×2 vs 3×3). Tests cross-benchmark gradient hypothesis.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_records_gsm8k\": 172800,\n      \"total_records_arc\": 172800,\n      \"total_records_hellaswag\": 172800,\n      \"models_completed_gsm8k\": 3,\n      \"models_completed_arc\": 2,\n      \"models_completed_hellaswag\": 3,\n      \"benchmarks_with_complete_data\": 3,\n      \"item_id_pct_gsm8k_pooled\": 0.143,\n      \"seed_pct_gsm8k\": 0.089,\n      \"temperature_pct_gsm8k\": 0.112,\n      \"prompt_pct_gsm8k\": 0.067,\n      \"seed_x_item_pct_gsm8k\": 0.138,\n      \"temperature_x_item_pct_gsm8k\": 0.124,\n      \"residual_pct_gsm8k\": 0.247,\n      \"precision_x_temperature_gsm8k_binary_pct\": 3.2,\n      \"g_coeff_binary_gsm8k_200item\": 0.198,\n      \"item_id_pct_arc_pooled\": 0.472,\n      \"seed_pct_arc\": 0.018,\n      \"temperature_pct_arc\": 0.031,\n      \"prompt_pct_arc\": 0.093,\n      \"residual_pct_arc\": 0.196,\n      \"precision_x_temperature_arc_binary_pct\": 0.4,\n      \"g_coeff_binary_arc_200item\": 0.311,\n      \"item_id_pct_hellaswag_pooled\": 0.387,\n      \"seed_pct_hellaswag\": 0.042,\n      \"temperature_pct_hellaswag\": 0.058,\n      \"prompt_pct_hellaswag\": 0.121,\n      \"precision_x_prompt_hellaswag_binary_pct\": 3.8,\n      \"residual_pct_hellaswag\": 0.203,\n      \"g_coeff_binary_hellaswag_200item\": 0.274,\n      \"cross_benchmark_gradient_pp\": 5.1,\n      \"item_id_ordering_by_benchmark\": \"ARC(47.2%) > HellaSwag(38.7%) > MMLU(~55%) > GSM8K(14.3%)\",\n      \"gradient_correlation_with_task_type\": -0.12,\n      \"direct_infra_x_method_binary_gsm8k\": 3.2,\n      \"direct_infra_x_method_binary_hellaswag\": 3.8,\n      \"direct_infra_x_method_binary_arc\": 0.4,\n      \"direct_infra_x_method_text_match_gsm8k\": 7.4,\n      \"direct_infra_x_method_text_match_hellaswag\": 5.9,\n      \"benchmarks_separability_fails_binary\": 2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: Variance Decomposition Across Benchmarks (Single-Model Pooled, Binary Scoring)\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows: 10 variance sources. Columns: MMLU, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. No systematic gradient visible. Item_id ranges from 14.3% (GSM8K) to 55%+ (MMLU). Residual uniformly large (19-25%). Interaction terms vary idiosyncratically.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: Infrastructure × Methodology Interactions (Binary + Text Match)\",\n        \"description\": \"All 2-way infra×method interactions per benchmark under both scoring metrics. Binary: GSM8K 3.2%, HellaSwag 3.8%, ARC 0.4%, MMLU 0.3%. Text match: GSM8K 7.4%, HellaSwag 5.9%. Separability fails on 2/4 benchmarks under binary.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6: G-coefficients by Benchmark and Metric\",\n        \"description\": \"Binary G: MMLU 0.36, ARC 0.31, HellaSwag 0.27, GSM8K 0.20. Text match G: MMLU 0.97, GSM8K 0.88, ARC 0.91, HellaSwag 0.93. Binary G is poor everywhere; text match is acceptable but variable.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The gradient narrative is dead. Variance structure is idiosyncratic per benchmark and not predictable from task type alone. This is actually a finding — \\\"variance structure is benchmark-specific\\\" — but it is a negative finding relative to the original framing. Separability fails on 2 benchmarks under binary scoring, undermining the confirmatory null that was supposed to validate isolated-factor studies. The paper must pivot: instead of \\\"here's a gradient that explains everything,\\\" the message becomes \\\"nothing is simple, everything interacts, and you cannot generalize reproducibility findings across benchmarks.\\\"\\n\\n\\nThe cross-benchmark gradient hypothesis collapses. The item_id ordering is MMLU(55%) > ARC(47%) > HellaSwag(39%) > GSM8K(14%), which does not follow any coherent task taxonomy. ARC (science reasoning) has higher item_id than HellaSwag (commonsense), despite commonsense items being more \\\"knowledge-like.\\\" GSM8K's low item_id (14.3%) is expected — math reasoning items have high seed×item and temperature×item interactions because chain-of-thought generation introduces stochastic branching at each reasoning step. But HellaSwag's intermediate position and ARC's high position are not predicted by any task-type theory. The correlation between task-type ranking and item_id ranking is near zero (r=-0.12), destroying the \\\"knowledge→reasoning gradient\\\" narrative.\\nSeparability fails on GSM8K (precision×temperature = 3.2% under binary) and HellaSwag (precision×prompt = 3.8%). The GSM8K failure is interpretable: at T=0.7, floating-point non-associativity in the softmax creates different token selection paths across precisions, and for multi-step reasoning, these divergences compound. The HellaSwag failure is less interpretable — precision×prompt interaction suggests that certain prompt framings expose precision-dependent attention patterns in the commonsense completion task. Neither failure was predicted by the MVP data (which showed separability under binary scoring on MMLU). This means the Claim 2 separability result is benchmark-conditional in a way that undermines confident generalization.\\nThe residual components are large everywhere (19.6-24.7%), foreshadowing the model×item interaction that will emerge when model is treated as a facet in exp-004.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-003 Multi-Hardware G-Study\",\n    \"description\": \"Multi-GPU type crossing to test hardware × methodology interaction. In the worst tier, second GPU type is unavailable (Manager cannot allocate datacenter GPU within timeline). Fallback: run on westd-32570 only with 2 GPU instances (same architecture) as pseudo-replication. Limited to same RTX PRO 6000 hardware.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"hardware_types_obtained\": 1,\n      \"second_gpu_available\": false,\n      \"fallback_design\": \"2 instances of RTX PRO 6000 (same arch, different physical units)\",\n      \"gpu_instance_x_method_interaction_pct\": 0.08,\n      \"gpu_instance_x_item_pct\": 0.03,\n      \"alt_second_gpu_type\": \"A100-80GB (if obtained)\",\n      \"alt_gpu_x_method_binary_pct\": 2.4,\n      \"alt_gpu_x_prompt_pct\": 1.8,\n      \"alt_gpu_x_temperature_pct\": 0.9,\n      \"alt_gpu_x_item_pct\": 3.1,\n      \"total_records_fallback\": 777600,\n      \"experiment_status\": \"degraded — same-architecture pseudo-replication only\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7a (fallback): Same-Architecture GPU Pseudo-Replication\",\n        \"description\": \"Variance decomposition showing gpu_instance contributes <0.1% across all interactions. Demonstrates within-architecture reproducibility is high but says nothing about cross-architecture effects. Footnote: \\\"True hardware crossing requires heterogeneous GPU architectures; this analysis uses same-architecture replication only.\\\"\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7b (alt): Cross-Architecture Variance Decomposition (if A100 obtained)\",\n        \"description\": \"GPU type as additional facet. GPU×prompt 1.8%, GPU×temperature 0.9%, GPU×item 3.1%. All exceed the 1% threshold used in MVP. Separability assumption for hardware is violated.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-003 either provides no useful data (fallback) or actively harmful data (alt). The fallback forces explicit scoping to single-architecture, which reviewers will flag. The alt scenario compounds the worst-tier narrative by adding yet another interacting facet. Either way, Claim 2 (separability) is damaged. The paper must reframe \\\"infrastructure\\\" as \\\"numerical precision\\\" only and acknowledge hardware as an uncontrolled confound.\\n\\n\\nIn the worst tier, exp-003 fails its primary objective. Two scenarios: (A) Hardware unavailable: Manager cannot allocate a second GPU architecture within the AAAI timeline. The fallback pseudo-replication (two physical units of RTX PRO 6000) shows near-zero cross-unit variance (0.08%), which is expected — same architecture, same drivers, same CUDA version. This tells us nothing about true hardware×methodology interaction. The \\\"infrastructure\\\" facet remains limited to \\\"numerical precision on a single GPU architecture,\\\" severely weakening Claim 2. (B) Hardware obtained but interaction is large: if an A100 is obtained, the GPU×method interaction reaches 2.4% under binary scoring (driven by different CUDA kernel implementations for attention between Ampere and Ada Lovelace). GPU×item interaction is even larger (3.1%), meaning hardware affects items differentially — some items are hardware-sensitive while others are not. This breaks the \\\"precision only\\\" scoping assumption and means the paper must either include hardware as a full facet (requiring much more analysis) or explicitly scope the study to single-architecture inference. Either outcome damages the paper.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-004 Comprehensive Analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Combined analysis of exp-001/001b/002/003 data. This is where the worst-tier narrative crystallizes. Model-as-facet reanalysis, bootstrap CIs, D-study projections, and all methodological defense analyses.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"item_id_pct_mmlu_model_as_facet\": 0.224,\n      \"model_pct_mmlu\": 0.068,\n      \"model_x_item_pct_mmlu\": 0.312,\n      \"prompt_x_item_pct_mmlu_model_facet\": 0.048,\n      \"temperature_x_item_pct_mmlu_model_facet\": 0.052,\n      \"residual_pct_mmlu_model_facet\": 0.119,\n      \"item_id_drop_from_single_to_facet_pp\": 33.6,\n      \"model_x_item_is_largest_source\": true,\n      \"item_id_pct_gsm8k_model_as_facet\": 0.091,\n      \"model_x_item_pct_gsm8k\": 0.287,\n      \"seed_x_item_pct_gsm8k_model_facet\": 0.112,\n      \"bootstrap_ci_item_id_mmlu_model_facet\": [\n        0.148,\n        0.301\n      ],\n      \"bootstrap_ci_model_x_item_mmlu\": [\n        0.238,\n        0.387\n      ],\n      \"bootstrap_ci_width_item_id_pp\": 15.3,\n      \"bootstrap_ci_width_model_x_item_pp\": 14.9,\n      \"bootstrap_ci_overlap_item_id_model_x_item\": true,\n      \"bootstrap_ci_item_id_gsm8k\": [\n        0.041,\n        0.142\n      ],\n      \"bootstrap_ci_model_x_item_gsm8k\": [\n        0.209,\n        0.364\n      ],\n      \"d_study_items_for_g90_mmlu_binary\": 1400,\n      \"d_study_items_for_g90_gsm8k_binary\": 2000,\n      \"d_study_items_for_g90_arc_binary\": 980,\n      \"d_study_items_for_g90_hellaswag_binary\": 1200,\n      \"d_study_items_for_g90_mmlu_text_match\": 45,\n      \"d_study_range_across_benchmarks\": \"45–2000 items\",\n      \"d_study_prompts_for_g90_mmlu_binary\": 8,\n      \"d_study_seeds_for_g90_mmlu_binary\": 4,\n      \"d_study_budget_mmlu_total_conditions\": 44800,\n      \"reml_vs_henderson_max_diff_pp\": 3.8,\n      \"reml_vs_henderson_rank_order_preserved\": false,\n      \"g_coeff_logprob_mmlu\": 0.412,\n      \"logprob_item_id_pct\": 0.483,\n      \"logprob_model_x_item_pct\": 0.198,\n      \"precision_x_temp_alters_rankings\": true,\n      \"ranking_instability_fraction\": 0.22,\n      \"three_way_interactions_total_pct\": 0.042,\n      \"irt_recommended_items_mmlu\": 110,\n      \"gtheory_recommended_items_mmlu\": 1400,\n      \"ratio_gtheory_to_irt\": 12.7,\n      \"min_detectable_interaction_pct\": 1.2,\n      \"power_for_2pct_interaction\": 0.89,\n      \"fixed_vs_random_max_diff_pp\": 7.2,\n      \"temperature_fixed_changes_item_id_by_pp\": 4.1,\n      \"bernoulli_variance_correlation_with_item_id\": 0.83,\n      \"mechanical_share_of_item_id\": 0.61,\n      \"bootstrap_jackknife_ci_ratio\": 1.34,\n      \"jackknife_ci_width_item_id_pp\": 11.4,\n      \"mmlu_3x3_vs_2x2_item_id_diff_pp\": 4.7,\n      \"mmlu_3x3_vs_2x2_changes_rank_order\": false,\n      \"g_coeff_binary_mmlu_14k_items_projected\": 0.891,\n      \"g_coeff_binary_mmlu_14k_items_ci\": [\n        0.847,\n        0.934\n      ],\n      \"compute_cost_recommended_budget_mmlu_usd\": 4200,\n      \"compute_cost_recommended_budget_gsm8k_usd\": 11500\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 8: Model-as-Facet Variance Decomposition (MMLU, binary scoring)\",\n        \"description\": \"The central table of the paper. Rows: item_id (22.4%), model (6.8%), model×item (31.2%), prompt×item (4.8%), temp×item (5.2%), precision×item (2.1%), seed (0.4%), prompt (1.2%), temperature (2.8%), precision (4.3%), 3-way+ interactions (4.2%), residual (11.9%). Model×item is largest. Bold. Adjacent column: bootstrap 95% CI for each. CIs on item_id and model×item overlap.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 9: Model-as-Facet Variance Decomposition Across Benchmarks\",\n        \"description\": \"Same format as Table 8 but columns = MMLU, GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag. Model×item is largest or co-largest on MMLU (31.2%) and GSM8K (28.7%). On ARC, item_id remains largest (34.1%) with model×item at 22.3%. HellaSwag: prompt×item (18.9%) competes with model×item (21.7%). No consistent pattern across benchmarks.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 10: D-Study Projections for G≥0.90 (Binary Scoring)\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows: MMLU, GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag. Columns: required items, prompts, seeds, total conditions, estimated GPU-hours, USD cost. Items range 980-2000. GSM8K marked \\\"exceeds available test items.\\\" Total costs: $4.2K-$11.5K per benchmark.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 11: G-theory vs IRT Item Recommendations\",\n        \"description\": \"Comparison table. IRT (tinyBenchmarks): 110 items. G-theory: 1400 items. Ratio: 12.7×. Explanation: IRT assumes fixed protocol; G-theory targets cross-protocol reliability. Both are \\\"correct\\\" for different reliability definitions.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 12: Bootstrap CI Widths by Variance Source and Benchmark\",\n        \"description\": \"All variance sources × all benchmarks. CI widths range 3-16pp. Sources with CI >12pp highlighted. Item_id and model×item exceed 12pp on all benchmarks. Precision main effect CIs are tighter (3-5pp). Message: only main effects can be estimated precisely; interactions and item-dependent sources are noisy.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 13: Methodological Sensitivity Analyses Summary\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows: REML vs Henderson, Fixed vs Random, Binary vs TextMatch, 3×3 vs 2×2, Bootstrap vs Jackknife. Columns: max difference (pp), rank order preserved (Y/N), conclusion. Two of five analyses show >5pp sensitivity. Overall: results are moderately robust but not insensitive to analytic choices.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-004 reveals that the entire single-model analysis framework was misleading. Model×item interaction (31.2%) — not item difficulty — is the dominant variance source when models are properly crossed. This is the worst-tier's central finding and it is genuinely novel and surprising. However, the wide bootstrap CIs, the Bernoulli mechanical artifact, and the benchmark-idiosyncratic patterns make the paper very hard to write cleanly. Every claim requires heavy qualification. The D-study budgets are impractically large and benchmark-specific, providing no actionable \\\"recipe.\\\" The G-theory vs IRT comparison is the strongest standalone contribution but is a methods comparison, not an empirical finding. The narrative must shift to: \\\"evaluation is harder than you think, and here's exactly why.\\\"\\n\\n\\nThe model-as-facet reanalysis is the earthquake. When model is added as a crossed facet to the MMLU G-study, item_id drops from ~55.8% (single-model) to 22.4%, while model×item interaction emerges at 31.2% — the LARGEST single variance source. This happens because different models find different items difficult: an item that Llama gets right 95% of the time, Mistral may get right only 40% of the time. This differential item functioning (DIF) was hidden in single-model analyses where it was confounded with the residual. The 33.6pp drop in item_id is dramatic and changes the entire paper narrative.\\nBootstrap CIs are wide (15.3pp for item_id, 14.9pp for model×item) and the two CIs overlap, meaning we cannot even definitively say which is larger. This is driven by item-level resampling with only 200 items — each bootstrap resample has ~127 unique items (Euler's formula), and the item×model interaction is sensitive to which items are included. The width exceeds 12pp on all benchmarks, making confident rank-ordering of variance sources impossible on most benchmarks.\\nD-study projections are dire: achieving G≥0.90 under binary scoring requires 1400 items on MMLU, 2000 on GSM8K (practically unreachable — GSM8K only has 1319 test items), and 980-1200 on ARC/HellaSwag. These budgets are 3-10× larger than MVP estimates because model×item interaction adds a variance source that cannot be reduced by prompt/seed averaging — only item expansion helps. The cross-benchmark range (45-2000 items) is so large that no single recommendation is actionable.\\nThe G-theory vs IRT comparison reveals a 12.7× discrepancy (tinyBenchmarks: 110 items; G-theory: 1400 items). This is because IRT ignores prompt/seed/temperature/ ordering facets entirely — it assumes a single fixed evaluation protocol. The G-theory budget accounts for reproducibility across ALL facets, making it much larger. This is actually a strong finding: IRT-based subsampling produces reliable rankings only within a fixed protocol, not across protocols.\\nFixed vs random sensitivity analysis (MD2) shows a 7.2pp maximum change when temperature is treated as fixed vs random. Temperature has only 2-3 design points, making the random assumption dubious. But the fixed treatment changes item_id by 4.1pp, which is large enough to affect conclusions. This is a genuine methodological vulnerability with no clean resolution.\\nThe Bernoulli correlation analysis (MD1) confirms that 61% of item_id's magnitude is mechanically driven by item difficulty spread through p(1-p). This is damning: more than half of the \\\"headline finding\\\" (item dominance) is a statistical artifact of using binary scoring on items with heterogeneous difficulty. The text_match metric does not suffer from this, but text_match has different (and less dramatic) variance structure.\\nEven projecting to 14K items (full MMLU), binary G reaches only 0.891 [0.847, 0.934], still below 0.90 with a CI that includes values as low as 0.847. This means even full MMLU may not provide reliable binary-scored evaluation when considering all facets — a provocative but hard-to-communicate finding.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-005 14B Model Extension\",\n    \"description\": \"Qwen3-14B and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B at BF16/FP16. Tests scale effects. Depends on exp-003 (hardware). In worst tier, hardware issues cascade.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"hardware_obtained\": false,\n      \"experiment_status\": \"blocked — requires ≥48GB VRAM datacenter GPU not available\",\n      \"alt_single_gpu_status\": \"attempted on westd-32570 (96GB) with reduced design\",\n      \"alt_total_records\": 28800,\n      \"alt_qwen14b_accuracy\": 0.738,\n      \"alt_deepseek_r1_distill_accuracy\": 0.712,\n      \"alt_item_id_pct_qwen14b\": 0.501,\n      \"alt_item_id_pct_deepseek_r1\": 0.423,\n      \"alt_cross_scale_item_correlation\": 0.64,\n      \"alt_model_x_item_estimated\": \"not estimable with 2-model design\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A1 (Appendix): 14B Model Accuracy Summary\",\n        \"description\": \"Minimal 2×2 table (2 models × 2 precisions). Qwen3-14B: 73.8%, DeepSeek-R1-Distill: 71.2%. Noted as \\\"reduced design, insufficient for G-theory analysis.\\\"\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-005 is the clearest casualty of the worst tier. It either doesn't run or produces too little data to support claims. Relegated to a brief appendix mention or dropped entirely. The cross-scale correlation finding (r=0.64) is interesting but requires follow-up work with more models and a full crossing design.\\n\\n\\nexp-005 is either blocked entirely (no datacenter GPU) or runs on the single available GPU with a severely reduced design. Even if it runs, 2 models is insufficient to estimate model×item interaction as a random effect — you need ≥3 for meaningful variance component estimation. The cross-scale item difficulty correlation (r=0.64 between 8B and 14B models) is lower than within-scale correlation (r=0.71-0.78 among 8B models), suggesting that scale increases model×item interaction further. This is consistent with the worst-tier narrative (larger models have more distinct item-level competence profiles) but the evidence is too thin to make claims. The experiment contributes almost nothing to the paper in the worst tier.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-006 Fine-Tuning G-Study\",\n    \"description\": \"RoBERTa-large on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA. 6 seeds × 3 learning rates × 4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task × 4 tasks = 1,728 total runs. Independent of inference experiments. Tests fine-tuning variance hierarchy.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_runs_completed\": 1614,\n      \"total_runs_planned\": 1728,\n      \"failure_rate\": 0.066,\n      \"failed_config\": \"lr=5e-4 × fp16 on RTE/COPA (small datasets, unstable)\",\n      \"item_id_pct_sst2\": 0.312,\n      \"seed_pct_sst2\": 0.089,\n      \"lr_pct_sst2\": 0.143,\n      \"data_shuffle_pct_sst2\": 0.034,\n      \"gpu_pct_sst2\": 0.002,\n      \"precision_pct_sst2\": 0.018,\n      \"seed_x_lr_pct_sst2\": 0.112,\n      \"residual_pct_sst2\": 0.178,\n      \"g_coeff_sst2\": 0.487,\n      \"item_id_pct_mnli\": 0.401,\n      \"lr_pct_mnli\": 0.098,\n      \"seed_pct_mnli\": 0.067,\n      \"seed_x_item_pct_mnli\": 0.134,\n      \"residual_pct_mnli\": 0.156,\n      \"g_coeff_mnli\": 0.534,\n      \"item_id_pct_rte\": 0.187,\n      \"seed_pct_rte\": 0.203,\n      \"lr_pct_rte\": 0.178,\n      \"seed_x_lr_pct_rte\": 0.156,\n      \"residual_pct_rte\": 0.214,\n      \"g_coeff_rte\": 0.298,\n      \"item_id_pct_copa\": 0.134,\n      \"seed_pct_copa\": 0.241,\n      \"lr_pct_copa\": 0.198,\n      \"residual_pct_copa\": 0.267,\n      \"g_coeff_copa\": 0.213,\n      \"inference_vs_finetuning_rank_correlation\": 0.31,\n      \"dominant_source_inference\": \"item_id (or model×item)\",\n      \"dominant_source_finetuning\": \"item_id on large datasets; seed on small datasets\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 14: Fine-Tuning Variance Decomposition Across Tasks\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows: seed, learning_rate, data_shuffle, gpu, precision, item_id, seed×lr, seed×item, residual. Columns: SST-2, MNLI, RTE, COPA. Clear split between large-dataset (item-dominated) and small-dataset (seed-dominated) patterns.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 15: Inference vs Fine-Tuning Variance Hierarchy Comparison\",\n        \"description\": \"Side-by-side ranking of variance sources. Inference: item_id > model×item > residual > temperature×item > prompt×item. Fine-tuning: item_id > seed×lr > seed > lr > residual. Different hierarchies, different implications.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-006 produces a valid standalone result (fine-tuning variance hierarchy differs from inference) but its contribution to the main paper is ambiguous. It supports the worst-tier message (\\\"nothing generalizes across paradigms\\\") but also weakens coherence — the paper is already struggling to find a unifying narrative, and adding a disconnected fine-tuning analysis makes it more scattered. Recommend relegating to a 1-page appendix or splitting into a separate short paper.\\n\\n\\nFine-tuning variance hierarchy differs substantially from inference. On large datasets (SST-2, MNLI), item_id is still the largest source (31-40%) but learning rate and seed×learning_rate interactions are substantial (10-14%), which have no analogue in inference. On small datasets (RTE: 277 items; COPA: 100 items), seed becomes dominant (20-24%) because the training dynamics are chaotic with few examples — small perturbations in initialization cascade through training.\\nGPU and precision contribute negligibly to fine-tuning variance (<2% combined), which contrasts with the 4-7% precision contribution in inference. This is because fine-tuning aggregates over many gradient steps, averaging out per-forward-pass precision effects.\\nThe cross-paradigm rank correlation (r=0.31) between inference and fine-tuning variance hierarchies means the findings do NOT transfer. A reviewer cannot extrapolate from our inference G-study to fine-tuning practice, and we cannot extrapolate from exp-006 back to inference. This supports the worst-tier narrative (\\\"nothing generalizes\\\") but also means exp-006 is somewhat disconnected from the paper's main story. It becomes an appendix-level extension rather than a core contribution.\\nThe 6.6% run failure rate (NaN loss on high-lr×fp16 combinations with small datasets) is a methodological nuisance. These failures are informative (they show that some precision×hyperparameter combinations are unstable) but create missing cells in the G-theory design, requiring imputation or reduced analysis.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-007 LayerCast Precision Mitigation\",\n    \"description\": \"LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as additional precision condition on MMLU. Tests whether this mixed-precision strategy reduces variance from the precision facet.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_records\": 43200,\n      \"layercast_accuracy\": 0.6482,\n      \"layercast_vs_fp32_gap_pp\": 4.88,\n      \"layercast_vs_bf16_gap_pp\": -3.62,\n      \"precision_variance_with_layercast_pct\": 5.1,\n      \"layercast_item_level_correlation_with_bf16\": 0.87,\n      \"layercast_item_level_correlation_with_fp32\": 0.72,\n      \"variance_reduction_from_layercast\": \"none — adds variance rather than reducing it\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A2 (Appendix): LayerCast Variance Impact\",\n        \"description\": \"4-row precision comparison (FP32, FP16, BF16, LayerCast) with accuracy, item-level correlation with BF16, and precision facet variance contribution. LayerCast is intermediate but not a clean fix.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-007 is a minor negative result. LayerCast doesn't serve as a precision mitigation strategy in this context. Useful as a 1-paragraph appendix note showing that mixed-precision approaches don't simplify the variance picture. Low priority for inclusion in the worst-tier paper.\\n\\n\\nLayerCast does not cleanly reduce variance because it creates a hybrid precision regime that behaves differently from both pure FP32 and pure FP16/BF16. The storage-in-FP16, compute-in-FP32 approach means attention computations use FP32 but weight quantization noise from FP16 storage propagates through. The result is an accuracy level between FP32 and BF16 (64.8%), but with item-level patterns that don't correlate perfectly with either pure precision. Adding LayerCast as a 4th precision level actually INCREASES precision facet variance (from 4.3% to 5.1%) because it adds another distinct level rather than collapsing precision differences. This is a negative result for the mitigation narrative but a positive result for the worst-tier message: precision effects are complex and non-monotonic, and simple mitigation strategies don't work as advertised.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-008 MATH Benchmark Extension\",\n    \"description\": \"MATH benchmark (200 items) with chain-of-thought reasoning. Tests whether reasoning-heavy tasks with long generation have qualitatively different variance structure.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_records\": 115200,\n      \"models_completed\": 2,\n      \"overall_accuracy_llama\": 0.341,\n      \"overall_accuracy_qwen\": 0.428,\n      \"accuracy_range_across_conditions\": 0.187,\n      \"item_id_pct_math\": 0.118,\n      \"seed_pct_math\": 0.134,\n      \"temperature_pct_math\": 0.156,\n      \"prompt_pct_math\": 0.087,\n      \"seed_x_item_pct_math\": 0.168,\n      \"temperature_x_item_pct_math\": 0.143,\n      \"precision_x_temperature_pct_math_binary\": 4.7,\n      \"residual_pct_math\": 0.194,\n      \"g_coeff_binary_math_200item\": 0.152,\n      \"d_study_items_for_g90_math_binary\": \"not achievable within MATH test set size\",\n      \"avg_generation_length_tokens\": 487,\n      \"generation_length_cv\": 0.68,\n      \"accuracy_conditional_on_cot_length\": \"non-monotonic — medium length CoT has highest accuracy\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A3 (Appendix): MATH Variance Decomposition\",\n        \"description\": \"Seed×item (16.8%) and temperature×item (14.3%) dominate. Item_id is only 11.8%. Precision×temperature is 4.7%. G=0.152. The most extreme interaction-dominated pattern in the study.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A4: Accuracy Range by Model Across Conditions (MATH)\",\n        \"description\": \"2 models × min/max/range across all conditions. Llama: 22.5-52.8% (range 30.3pp). Qwen: 31.2-58.4% (range 27.2pp). Demonstrates that single-run MATH evaluation is unreliable.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"MATH is the poster child for the worst-tier narrative: everything interacts, nothing is stable, and single-run evaluation is meaningless. But with only 2 models and a reduced design, the evidence is thin. The extreme interaction dominance is compelling but also predictable (CoT reasoning is inherently stochastic). The finding reinforces the \\\"no gradient\\\" message — MATH is not simply \\\"one step further\\\" on a knowledge→reasoning continuum; it has a qualitatively different variance structure that shares nothing with MMLU or ARC. Best used as a dramatic closing example in the paper rather than a core analysis.\\n\\nMATH represents the extreme end of the worst-tier pattern. Item_id is only 11.8% — the lowest of any benchmark — because chain-of-thought generation introduces massive stochastic branching. Each reasoning step is a decision point where temperature and seed can send the model down different solution paths. The seed×item (16.8%) and temperature×item (14.3%) interactions are the dominant sources, meaning the same item can be solved or not depending on the random seed and temperature — an extreme form of the model×item interaction pattern.\\nThe 18.7pp accuracy range across conditions for the same model on the same items is striking: the \\\"same\\\" evaluation can produce 34.1% or 52.8% accuracy for Llama on MATH depending on prompt/seed/temperature. This makes any single-run MATH evaluation essentially meaningless for fine-grained model comparison.\\nSeparability fails catastrophically: precision×temperature interaction is 4.7% under binary scoring, the highest of any benchmark. This is because FP16/BF16 rounding in the softmax creates different token selections at each reasoning step, and at higher temperatures these divergences compound over 400+ tokens of chain-of-thought.\\nG=0.152 for 200 items under binary scoring is the lowest across all benchmarks. D-study projections show G≥0.90 is not achievable within MATH's test set (5000 items) because the dominant variance sources (seed×item, temperature×item) cannot be reduced by adding items — they require averaging over many seeds and temperatures per item, which is computationally expensive.\\nMistral's exclusion (accuracy <10%, effectively random guessing on MATH) further reduces model coverage to 2, making model-as-facet analysis impossible on this benchmark.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":404,"startLine":1,"totalLines":404}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SUHFgdAin7xXBFyW23iMMV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have both files. Let me also read the distilled research and writing skills to inform the paper style.\n\nActually, the instructions say to write the paper directly. Let me synthesize what I've read and write the LaTeX paper.\n\nKey narrative points from the guidance:\n1. Core framing: \"Model×item interaction is the hidden dominant variance source\" — not item difficulty alone\n2. Separability failure becomes a positive finding\n3. D-study: \"current evaluation practices are even more inadequate than prior work suggests\"\n4. Drop gradient narrative; replace with \"variance structure is benchmark-specific and unpredictable\"\n\n\n5. The paper becomes more iconoclastic, targeting ACL ARR October\n\nI'm structuring this with an abstract, introduction, related work, method section covering the G-theory framework and experimental design, then all eight experiments with corresponding tables, followed by results and discussion. I need to ground every quantitative claim in the experiment data, build tables for each experiment, and maintain the iconoclastic tone throughout—emphasizing that model-by-item interactions dominate and current evaluation practices are fundamentally inadequate.\n\nWriting the full LaTeX paper now...\n\nNow I'm organizing the tables across all experiments: variance decomposition tables for single models and across benchmarks, precision-temperature-model interactions, item resampling stability, infrastructure effects, GPU pseudo-replication results, the central model-as-facet analysis with D-study projections, and G-coefficients by benchmark. This table structure will support the narrative that complexity and interactions are everywhere.\n\nFor the paper itself, I need to be selective about what goes in the main body versus the appendix. The key tables are the single-model variance baseline to establish the misleading picture, then the model-as-facet decomposition to show the dramatic shift when treating models as a random factor rather than fixed.\n\nCross-benchmark results demonstrate that these patterns aren't just MMLU artifacts, and the infrastructure-methodology interactions reveal why we can't easily separate these design choices. The D-study projections show the practical implications of these variance structures, and comparing G-theory to IRT grounds the findings in established measurement theory.\n\nFor the appendix, I'm organizing supporting analyses: item resampling stability, accuracy breakdowns by different settings, G-coefficients across benchmarks, bootstrap confidence intervals, sensitivity checks, fine-tuning variance, inference versus fine-tuning comparisons, results from the 14B model, LayerCast impacts, and MATH benchmark decompositions.\n\nNow I'm structuring the paper itself using standard conference formatting. The narrative arc starts by establishing why evaluation reproducibility matters and how prior work has tackled individual factors in isolation. The key contribution is applying G-theory across all factors simultaneously, which reveals that single-model analyses fundamentally misrepresent the variance structure. When I add model as a facet, the model-by-item interaction emerges as a dominant source of variance.\n\nThe critical insight is that items don't have inherent difficulty—they're easy for some models and hard for others, and this interaction persists regardless of prompt standardization or temperature adjustments. It only shrinks with dramatically larger item pools. On most benchmarks, infrastructure and methodology interactions also exceed acceptable thresholds, meaning isolated-factor studies can't capture the full picture. The D-study calculations show that reaching acceptable generalizability requires 980 to 2000 items depending on the benchmark, which exceeds what's available for some datasets like GSM8K. Current evaluation practices fall far short of these requirements. The 12.7× discrepancy with IRT stems from IRT's inability to account for protocol-level variation.\n\nNow I'm structuring the paper with a standard LaTeX template, starting with the abstract and introduction, then moving through related work, the generalizability theory framework, experimental design details, and results sections that highlight the misleading baseline findings, the dominant model-item interactions, and benchmark-specific variance patterns.\n\nI'll extract the specific metrics from the experiment data—three models tested across hundreds of thousands of records with varying accuracy scores—and weave those into the results narrative. Now I'm looking at the variance decomposition across different benchmarks—GSM8K shows item selection dominates at 14.3%, while ARC and HellaSwag have much higher item effects around 47% and 39% respectively. The Gini coefficients cluster around 0.2-0.36 depending on the benchmark, and I'm noticing that separability breaks down on GSM8K and HellaSwag when examining interaction effects between precision and temperature or prompt variables.\n\nIn the central experiment, when I model with item_id as a facet alongside the model effect, there's a dramatic drop in item variance from 55.8% down to 22.4%—that 33.6 percentage point shift is substantial. The model×item interaction becomes the largest component at 31.2%, with bootstrap confidence intervals showing overlap between item_id and model×item estimates, suggesting these effects are competing for variance explanation. I'm also preparing a D-study to generalize these findings.\n\nLooking at the reliability thresholds across benchmarks, I need around 1400 items for MMLU, 2000 for GSM8K, 980 for ARC, and 1200 for HellaSwag to reach 0.90 generalizability. There's a notable discrepancy between G-theory and IRT approaches—a 12.7× difference in required sample sizes—and the estimation method matters too, with REML versus Henderson showing up to 3.8 percentage points difference and not even preserving rank order. The Bernoulli correlation with item_id is quite high at 0.83, though mechanical factors account for about 61% of that. When I project the binary MMLU reliability to 14K items using G-theory, I get 0.891 with a confidence interval of [0.847, 0.934].\n\nThe follow-up experiments show mixed results: exp-005 had some blocking issues but alternative models like Qwen14B and DeepSeek-R1 performed at 73.8% and 71.2% respectively, with cross-scale item correlation around 0.64. I'm starting to look at SST-2 results in exp-006.\n\nNow examining the variance decomposition across different tasks—SST-2 shows item_id accounting for 31.2% of variance with a generalizability coefficient of 0.487, while MNLI has higher item effects at 40.1% and better reliability at 0.534. RTE and COPA show much lower generalizability (0.298 and 0.213) with seed effects becoming more dominant. The inference versus fine-tuning rank correlation is quite weak at 0.31. LayerCast in exp-007 achieves 0.6482 accuracy but doesn't actually reduce precision variance, and in exp-008 I'm breaking down the MATH task variance across item_id, seed, temperature, and prompt factors.\n\nContinuing with the interaction effects and model comparisons...\n\nNow I'm thinking about how to structure the paper itself. For a conference submission like ACL or AAAI with their standard 8-page limits, I need to be strategic about what goes in the main body versus appendix. I'll write this as a complete paper with all the essential sections and results tables, using LaTeX with a two-column format to match typical conference layouts. For the bibliography, I'll use the standard thebibliography environment to keep everything self-contained. Writing the paper now...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4873,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12794,"cache_read_input_toke